days. Tweed Day was big, as was Uncommon Instrument Awareness Day. Today happens to be National Watermelon Day. You didn’t know?', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/08/03/daily-report-small-holidays-play-big-on-social-media/', 'snippet': 'Looking for something to say, many social media users are timing posts to match novel holidays. Tweed Day was big, as was Uncommon Instrument Awareness Day. Today happens to be National Watermelon Day. You didn’t know?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Apparently, where there’s a will, there’s a day. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/11/30/technology/bits-daily-report/bits-daily-report-articleLarge-v4.png', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/11/30/technology/bits-daily-report/bits-daily-report-articleLarge-v4.png', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/11/30/technology/bits-daily-report/bits-daily-report-jumbo-v6.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/11/30/technology/bits-daily-report/bits-daily-report-superJumbo-v3.png', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/11/30/technology/bits-daily-report/bits-daily-report-thumbStandard-v5.png', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/11/30/technology/bits-daily-report/bits-daily-report-thumbStandard-v5.png', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/11/30/technology/bits-daily-report/bits-daily-report-thumbLarge-v5.png', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Daily Report: Small Holidays Play Big on Social Media', 'kicker': 'Bits', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Social Media', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-08-03T12:43:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By Joseph Plambeck', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joseph', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Plambeck', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/31d0719c-51c2-54b4-a859-786239edab65', 'word_count': 140, 'uri': 'nyt://article/31d0719c-51c2-54b4-a859-786239edab65'}, {'abstract': 'Who’s responsible for the gap?', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/08/03/cap-and-trade-and-polarization/', 'snippet': 'Who’s responsible for the gap?', 'lead_paragraph': 'One of the most obvious facts about the U.S. political scene is also a fact most pundits refuse to acknowledge: the extreme polarization we now experience, the complete disappearance of any kind of political center, is not a two-sided phenomenon. Democrats haven’t moved drastically to the left — if anything they inched right for a couple of decades, and are only now shuffling slightly back toward a more robust liberalism. But Republicans have barreled off to the right.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Cap and Trade and Polarization', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2015-08-03T12:44:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/86d56aa8-5e79-5523-8ca3-8b1b4bac6b6a', 'word_count': 288, 'uri': 'nyt://article/86d56aa8-5e79-5523-8ca3-8b1b4bac6b6a'}, {'abstract': 'It seems like a missed opportunity that designers have shown little interest in dressing top female athletes like the mixed martial arts star.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/08/04/fashion/when-will-fashion-discover-ronda-rousey.html', 'snippet': 'It seems like a missed opportunity that designers have shown little interest in dressing top female athletes like the mixed martial arts star.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For a sector that has suddenly discovered its love of all things “athleisure” (Tory Sport, anyone? Stella McCartney for Adidas? Kering’s Sport & Lifestyle division?) — not to mention its love of all things celebrity — fashion has been extraordinarily slow to embrace female sports stars.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/04/world/04OTR-web/04OTR-web-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 737, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/08/04/world/04OTR-web/04OTR-web-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 737}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/04/world/04OTR-web/04OTR-web-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 834, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/04/world/04OTR-web/04OTR-web-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1668, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/04/world/04OTR-web/04OTR-web-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/08/04/world/04OTR-web/04OTR-web-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/04/world/04OTR-web/04OTR-web-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'When Will Fashion Discover Ronda Rousey?', 'kicker': 'On the Runway', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rousey, Ronda', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fashion and Apparel', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mixed Martial Arts', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ADVERTISING AND MARKETING', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Athletics and Sports', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-08-03T12:48:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Styles', 'section_name': 'Fashion & Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Vanessa Friedman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Vanessa', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Friedman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/de0a49a1-12c5-5081-bf2e-dc9daa45055b', 'word_count': 460, 'uri': 'nyt://article/de0a49a1-12c5-5081-bf2e-dc9daa45055b'}, {'abstract': 'Yes, there is none.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/08/03/no-sanity-clause/', 'snippet': 'Yes, there is none.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Just in case you had any illusions about the state of US governance, and the sanity of the party that now controls Congress, the estimable Stan Collender now tells us that there is a better-than-even chance of a government shutdown this fall. And the issue that will bring us to the brink? Republican demands that we defund Planned Parenthood.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'No Sanity Clause', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2015-08-03T12:53:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/601a0f21-79fe-56a7-a523-d954445889f5', 'word_count': 59, 'uri': 'nyt://article/601a0f21-79fe-56a7-a523-d954445889f5'}, {'abstract': 'A New York regulator unearthed internal emails between Promontory Financial and the British bank Standard Chartered that call the firm’s independence into question.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2015/08/03/business/dealbook/document-report-suspending-wall-street-consulting-firm.html', 'snippet': 'A New York regulator unearthed internal emails between Promontory Financial and the British bank Standard Chartered that call the firm’s independence into question.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A New York regulator unearthed internal emails between Promontory Financial and the British bank Standard Chartered that call the firm’s independence into question.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/03/business/document-report-suspending-wall-street-consulting-firm/document-report-suspending-wall