, 'lead_paragraph': 'More than 80 percent of neonatal intensive-care units, or NICUs, use temporary first names for patients — Babygirl Jackson or Babyboy Goldsmith, for example — a convention that may lead to errors in prescribing medicines. A new study has found that a simple change in this procedure can significantly reduce such errors.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/04/26/health/well-risks/well-risks-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 215, 'width': 215, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/04/26/health/well-risks/well-risks-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 215, 'width': 215, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/04/26/health/well-risks/well-risks-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/04/26/health/well-risks/well-risks-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/04/26/health/well-risks/well-risks-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Baby Name Change Could Reduce Hospital Errors', 'kicker': 'Well', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Pregnancy: A Way to Reduce NICU Errors', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Babies and Infants', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hospitals', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Names, Personal', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N