kistan, where rights groups portrayed it as a stark example of a flawed judicial system.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/08/05/world/asia/pakistan-hangs-shafqat-hussain-inmate-said-to-have-been-tortured-into-confessing.html', 'snippet': 'The case of Shafqat Hussain had become a cause célèbre in Pakistan, where rights groups portrayed it as a stark example of a flawed judicial system.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ISLAMABAD, Pakistan — A man whose lawyers said he had been tortured into confessing to murder, and who they said was a minor at the time of the crime, was hanged early Tuesday, despite pleas from rights groups in Pakistan and overseas.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Pakistan Hangs Inmate Said to Have Been Tortured Into Confessing', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Pakistan Hangs Inmate Who Is Said to Have Confessed Under Torture', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Pakistan', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Capital Punishment', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hussain, Shafqat', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Karachi (Pakistan)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Human Rights and Human Rights Violations', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Confessions', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AMNESTY INTERNATIONAL', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Endowment for Democracy', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Supreme Court of Pakistan', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-08-04T08:30:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Salman Masood', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Salman', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Masood', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/09ab76a2-2bb8-5c44-8309-92736264a4a1', 'word_count': 539, 'uri': 'nyt://article/09ab76a2-2bb8-5c44-8309-92736264a4a1'}, {'abstract': 'How are wildfires being dealt with in the American West?', 'web_url': 'https://learning.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/08/04/news-qs-wildfires-blaze-in-california/', 'snippet': 'How are wildfires being dealt with in the American West?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Note: We’ve updated 6 Q’s About the News for the new school year. This feature, now known as News Q’s, will be a daily mini-lesson, with both Before Reading and After Reading questions, and a suggested activity for going further. Let us know what you think.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'News Q’s | Wildfires Blaze in California', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2015-08-04T08:49:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By Shannon Doyne', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Shannon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Doyne', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6a6fd8ec-485f-5144-a9c9-bc3f02744b32', 'word_count': 342, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6a6fd8ec-485f-5144-a9c9-bc3f02744b32'}, {'abstract': 'Percy believed that we feel good when we no longer feel uncertain about our role in the world; when everyone is focused, connected and engaged.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/08/09/books/review/walker-percys-theory-of-hurricanes.html', 'snippet': 'Percy believed that we feel good when we no longer feel uncertain about our role in the world; when everyone is focused, connected and engaged.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Walker Percy had a theory about hurricanes. “Though science taught that good environments were better than bad environments, it appeared to him that the opposite was the case,” he wrote of Will Barrett, the semi-autobiographical title character of his second novel, “The Last Gentleman.” “Take hurricanes, for example, certainly a bad environment if ever there was one. It was his impression that not just he but other people felt better in hurricanes.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '32', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/02/books/review/02isaacson/02isaacson-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 907, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/08/02/books/review/02isaacson/02isaacson-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 907}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/02/books/review/02isaacson/02isaacson-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 678, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/02/books/review/02isaacson/02isaacson-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1355, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/02/books/review/02isaacson/02isaacson-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/08/02/books/review/02isaacson/02isaacson-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/02/books/review/02isaacson/02isaacson-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Walker Percy’s Theory of Hurricanes ', 'kicker': 'Critic’s Take', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Walker Percy’s Theory of Hurricanes ', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hurricane Katrina (2005)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Percy, Walker', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hurricanes and Tropical Storms', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-08-04T09:00:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Walter Isaacson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Walter', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Isaacson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5baeb638-1961-5d63-9eab-d24fde94fa2d', 'word_count': 1243, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5baeb638-1961-5d63-9eab-d24fde94fa2d'}, {'abstract': 'This debut novel explores existential questions through the experiences of an administrative worker.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/08/09/books/review/the-beautiful-bureaucrat-by-helen-phillips.html', 'snippet': 'This debut novel explores existential questions through the experiences of an administrative worker.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the opening pages of Camus’s “The Stranger,” a nurse warns Meursault to keep a steady pace during his mother’s funeral procession: “If you go slowly,” she says, “you risk getting sunstroke. But if you go too fast, you work up a sweat and then catch a chill inside the church.” Meursault’s response conveys his overwhelming, and baffling, sense of imprisonment: “She was right. There was no way out.” It’s an illustrative moment of the paralysis — damned if I do, damned if I don’t — that characterizes so much of existential literature: Vladimir and Estragon, waiting for a God who never shows; the man from the country in the parable told by the priest in Kafka’s “The Trial,” inert, cursing his fate and praying to fleas, waiting for the doorkeeper to admit him into the Law; Gregor Samsa, trapped between identities, unable to leave his house or return to his human condition. This existential paralysis emerges, in part, from spiritual crisis: If God is dead, what is the individual’s role in an essentially meaningless universe? Are we pawns in the thrall of bureaucratic (Kafka) or totalitarian (Orwell) systems? Or are we, in fact, the ones with ultimate power; the arbiters — even unknowingly — of life and death?', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/09/books/review/09QUATTRO/09QUATTRO-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 720, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/08/09/books/review/09QUATTRO/09QUATTRO-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 720}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/09/books/review/09QUATTRO/09QUATTRO-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 854, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/09/books/review/09QUATTRO/09QUATTRO-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1092, 'width': 910, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/09/books/review/09QUATTRO/09QUATTRO-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnai