'value': 'Roman Catholic Church', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New Hampshire', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New Jersey', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-08-04T18:25:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Politics', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By First Draft', 'person': [{'firstname': 'First', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Draft', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b7c88045-99cc-5dc0-908b-c6b54b3cd795', 'word_count': 38, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b7c88045-99cc-5dc0-908b-c6b54b3cd795'}, {'abstract': 'Bar Goto gives cocktails an Asian spin; wood-fired ovens at Macchina; Indian flavorings at Madison Square Tavern, and more.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/08/05/dining/creperie-delice-and-sarrasin-opens.html', 'snippet': 'Bar Goto gives cocktails an Asian spin; wood-fired ovens at Macchina; Indian flavorings at Madison Square Tavern, and more.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Délice & Sarrasin Hunger for authentic French crepes drove Christophe Caron, a model, to open this creperie. Mr. Caron enlisted his mother, Yvette, a chef and former surgeon, and his father, Patrick, an engineer, in the project. Now for the French lesson: The word sarrasin means “buckwheat” and refers to the flour traditionally used for savory crepes, or galettes as they are called in France. (Sweet crepes are made with white flour.) And at this charming little cafe, both varieties are well balanced. None of the filling combinations, including smoked salmon with shrimp, or chocolate and banana, overburden the flavor of the delicate crepe. Pastries, coffee drinks, wine and cider are also served: 20 Christopher Street (Gay Street), 212-243-7200, delicesarrasin.com.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multim