e': 'organizations', 'value': 'Facebook Inc', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-08-06T17:34:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kathryn Varn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kathryn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Varn', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ee4bf249-3cca-5374-8e47-17aaa6a49309', 'word_count': 362, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ee4bf249-3cca-5374-8e47-17aaa6a49309'}, {'abstract': 'Plus: how to say yes after saying no; when the boss infringes on your down time; and standing up to a difficult cousin.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/08/09/fashion/what-dad-should-do-about-the-brat-pack.html', 'snippet': 'Plus: how to say yes after saying no; when the boss infringes on your down time; and standing up to a difficult cousin.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I am a 65-year-old man, vigorous and still working. I divorced 20 years ago when I came out as a gay man. After years of dating, I find myself in love with a man in his early 20s who loves me back. We have been dating for a couple of years. I do not support him financially; he is not in my will. But my three adult children, all married, find the age difference impossible to accept. I was their primary caretaker, and we have always been close. I don’t want a rift. How to proceed?', 'print_section': 'ST', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/28/fashion/social_inline/social_inline-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 599, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/28/fashion/social_inline/social_inline-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 599}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'cred