', 'byline': {'original': 'By A.O. Scott', 'person': [{'firstname': 'A.', 'middlename': 'O.', 'lastname': 'Scott', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/dbc9f9a7-2483-5456-9f16-b568f85d997c', 'word_count': 832, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dbc9f9a7-2483-5456-9f16-b568f85d997c'}, {'abstract': 'The Santa Fe Opera said its next commissioned work would be “The (R)evolution of Steve Jobs,” which will receive its first workshop in September in San Francisco.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/08/07/arts/music/a-steve-jobs-opera-is-up-next-for-the-santa-fe-company.html', 'snippet': 'The Santa Fe Opera said its next commissioned work would be “The (R)evolution of Steve Jobs,” which will receive its first workshop in September in San Francisco.', 'lead_paragraph': 'SANTA FE, N.M. — Just don’t call it iOpera. The Santa Fe Opera has announced that its next commissioned work will be “The (R)evolution of Steve Jobs,” with music by Mason Bates, known for combining electronic sounds and traditional instruments, and a libretto by Mark Campbell.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Steve Jobs Opera Is Up Next for the Santa Fe Company', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Up Next: Opera About Steve Jobs', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Woolfe, Zachary', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Opera', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Santa Fe Opera', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bates, Mason', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Campbell, Mark (Librettist)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jobs, Steven P', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The (R)evolution of Steve Jobs (Opera)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-08-06T22:03:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Zachary Woolfe', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Zachary', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Woolfe', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5a121242-4117-5670-aa18-6abb0feeb081', 'word_count': 236, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5a121242-4117-5670-aa18-6abb0feeb081'}, {'abstract': 'A modern “Odyssey,” this opera, based on Charles Frazier’s 1997 novel, is about a wounded Civil War soldier who escapes a Confederate hospital and walks nearly 300 miles home.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/08/07/arts/music/review-cold-mountain-at-santa-fe-opera-recounts-a-separated-lovers-arduous-journey-only-one-half-makes-the-journey.html', 'snippet': 'A modern “Odyssey,” this opera, based on Charles Frazier’s 1997 novel, is about a wounded Civil War soldier who escapes a Confederate hospital and walks nearly 300 miles home.', 'lead_paragraph': 'SANTA FE, N.M. — It’s quite a ways from here to western North Carolina, where Charles Frazier set his 1997 novel, “Cold Mountain.” And it’s quite a different landscape out there, too.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/07/arts/07COLDMOUNTAIN1/07COLDMOUNTAIN1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 801, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/08/07/arts/07COLDMOUNTAIN1/07COLDMOUNTAIN1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 801}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/07/arts/07COLDMOUNTAIN1/07COLDMOUNTAIN1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'w