ght for seriousness and the non-Jeb, non-Donald field.', 'web_url': 'https://douthat.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/08/07/the-republican-scrum-begins/', 'snippet': 'In spite of Trump, a pretty good night for seriousness and the non-Jeb, non-Donald field.', 'lead_paragraph': 'My fantasy of the 2016 G.O.P. primary debate, regular readers may remember, involved a serious clash of ideas between Marco Rubio and Rand Paul, the two candidates who seemed\xa0— as of six months ago, at least\xa0— to be charting\xa0the most creative and interesting paths forward for a Republican Party that’s lost the popular vote in five of the last six presidential contests. My worst-case scenario, on the other hand, basically involved a replay of 2012 — an establishment candidate playing it as safe as possible while a group of would-be populists fought one another noisily but emptily for the chance to finish second.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Republican Scrum Begins', 'kicker': 'Ross Douthat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2016', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bush, Jeb', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Carson, Benjamin S', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Christie, Christopher J', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cruz, Ted', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Huckabee, Mike', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kasich, John R', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Paul, Rand', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rubio, Marco', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Fox News Channel', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Scotland', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-08-07T12:47:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ross Douthat', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ross', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Douthat', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a6af97cd-5a00-5e3e-bbb2-411ab5805127', 'word_count': 737, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a6af97cd-5a00-5e3e-bbb2-411ab5805127'}, {'abstract': 'With the increased popularity of online shopping, New York co-ops, condos and rental buildings are being forced to reconsider their delivery policies.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/08/09/realestate/the-doormans-dilemma-what-to-do-with-all-those-packages.html', 'snippet': 'With the increased popularity of online shopping, New York co-ops, condos and rental buildings are being forced to reconsider their delivery policies.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As president of the board of his Midtown East co-op, Dennis Paget has reason to walk a little faster when a particular fellow resident comes into view. To do otherwise would mean enduring yet another stern lecture about the building’s supposedly dilatory parcel delivery service.', 'print_section': 'RE', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/09/realestate/09PACKAGE1/09PACKAGE1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/08/09/realestate/09PACKAGE1/09PACKAGE1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/09/realestate/09PACKAGE1/09PACKAGE1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/09/realestate/09PACKAGE1/09PACKAGE1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/09/realestate/09PACKAGE1/09PACKAGE1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/08/09/realestate/09PACKAGE1/09PACKAGE1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/09/realestate/09PACKAGE1/09PACKAGE1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Doorman’s Dilemma: What to Do With All Those Packages?', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'When Every Day Seems Like Christmas ', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Delivery Services', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Real Estate and Housing (Residential)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Building Service Employees', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Doormen', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-08-07T12:57:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'RealEstate', 'section_name': 'Real Estate', 'byline': {'original': 'By Joanne Kaufman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joanne', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kaufman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4a3809a0-7866-5ed1-a2e6-26a3ae37cba3', 'word_count': 1326, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4a3809a0-7866-5ed1-a2e6-26a3ae37cba3'}, {'abstract': 'This debut novel centers on an outsize food prodigy.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/08/09/books/review/kitchens-of-the-great-midwest-by-j-ryan-stradal.html', 'snippet': 'This debut novel centers on an outsize food prodigy.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Larger than life” is usually a cliché, but in the case of Eva Thorvald — the 6-foot-2 mighty Aphrodite who strides through the pages of J. Ryan Stradal’s appealing first novel — the description is literally as well as metaphorically accurate. Born with a “once-in-a-generation palate,” Eva is a food prodigy. At 3½ months old, she destroys a vegetable stand in pursuit of a perfect heirloom tomato; at 10, she runs a cottage industry from her bedroom closet, growing chocolate habanero peppers so incendiary that her client, the city’s best Mexican restaurant, is forced to post a warning label on the menu; at 11, she earns extra cash from bar bets by consuming quantities of blistering chili and “Circle of Hell” chicken wings. Yet despite a life pockmarked by poverty and other adversities, Eva has an equally outsize heart. A warring mass of desires, talents and imperfections, she’s an attractively flawed, completely likable demigoddess.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/09/books/review/09DRZAL/09DRZAL-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 319, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/08/09/books/review/09DRZAL/09DRZAL-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 319}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/09/books/review/09DRZAL/09DRZAL-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 545, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, '