uters', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mac OS (Operating System)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-08-07T13:36:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'subsection_name': 'Personal Tech', 'byline': {'original': 'By J. D. Biersdorfer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'J.', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Question', '_id': 'nyt://article/a7fd5204-6108-59f9-ac29-a4c9bb6a4d95', 'word_count': 603, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a7fd5204-6108-59f9-ac29-a4c9bb6a4d95'}, {'abstract': 'C.J. Box is known for his thrillers set in Wyoming, but in “Badlands,” No. 5 on the hardcover fiction list, he shifts his attention to North Dakota and the fracking boom.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/08/16/books/review/inside-the-list.html', 'snippet': 'C.J. Box is known for his thrillers set in Wyoming, but in “Badlands,” No. 5 on the hardcover fiction list, he shifts his attention to North Dakota and the fracking boom.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Carbon Footprints: The thriller writer C.J. Box (whose initials stand for Charles James, dashing your hopes that his full name was Cracker Jack Box) is best known for his series of gritty mysteries about the Wyoming game warden Joe Pickett. But in “Badlands,” a stand-alone suspense thriller new at No.\u202f5 on the hardcover fiction list, Box shifts his attention to North Dakota and the fracking boom; the book, set in the fictional oil town of Grimstad, N.D., is about a 12-year-old boy who witnesses a car accident on his paper route and makes off with the bundle of drugs and money he finds at the scene. As a child, Box had relatives who worked blue-collar jobs in the energy industry — “The house I grew up in didn’t have a single bookcase except mine,” he once told a website devoted to crime fiction — and in this novel he pokes some fun at journalists who reflexively condemn big oil. “They fly all the way here from the East Coast burning up jet fuel,” the town sheriff grouses at one point, “then rent cars and fill ’em with gasoline to drive all the way to Grimstad just so they can trot me out to say, ‘Oil is bad.’ I won’t play their game anymore.” Box has observed reporters up close: He used to be one. “My first job was working for a small Wyoming weekly newspaper,” he told the same website, The Rap Sheet, in 2010. “In retrospect, it was spectacular training in ‘the real world’ for what was to come. I learned so much, and met so many people in all walks of life. Much of what I’ve since written came from those days as a starving reporter.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/09/books/review/09insidelist-box/09insidelist-box-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 275, 'width': 288, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/09/books/review/09insidelist-box/09insidelist-box-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 275, 'width': 288, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/09/books/review/09insidelist-box/09insidelist-box-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/08/09/books/review/09insidelist-box/09insidelist-box-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/09/books/review/09insidelist-box/09insidelist-box-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Inside the List', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Inside the List', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-08-07T13:45:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gregory Cowles', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gregory', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cowles', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/69e998f6-bf92-5c4e-9dbe-26c37bd7274b', 'word_count': 522, 'uri': 'nyt://article/69e998f6-bf92-5c4e-9dbe-26c37bd7274b'}, {'abstract': 'Many of the stories in Valerie Martin’s collection — which looks at artists, nature and transition — are set in New Orleans.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/08/09/books/review/sea-lovers-selected-stories-by-valerie-martin.html', 'snippet': 'Many of the stories in Valerie Martin’s collection — which looks at artists, nature and transition — are set in New Orleans.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Among the many potent themes that weave in and out of the stories in “Sea Lovers” — the stark realities of the artist’s path; the proximity of death to life; the complexities of the relationship between humans and animals — are a number of seemingly ordinary yet revelatory \xadimages. For Valerie Martin’s characters, something as simple as drinking a glass of water can yield a moment of reflection and clarity.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '21', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/09/books/review/09brownrigg/09brownrigg-articleLarge.jpg', 'h