': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/09/books/review/09LOYD/0809-BKS-Loyd-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1036, 'width': 1654, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/09/books/review/09LOYD/0809-BKS-Loyd-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/08/09/books/review/09LOYD/0809-BKS-Loyd-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/09/books/review/09LOYD/0809-BKS-Loyd-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘The Little Brother,’ by Victoria Patterson', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Brother, Who Art Thou?', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Patterson, Victoria', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Little Brother (Book)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-08-07T15:15:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Amy Grace Loyd', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Amy', 'middlename': 'Grace', 'lastname': 'Loyd', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/073c7357-3e31-5143-8fe1-24cde0d5e118', 'word_count': 616, 'uri': 'nyt://article/073c7357-3e31-5143-8fe1-24cde0d5e118'}, {'abstract': 'Do any of the Republican candidates support policies that support working families? We don’t know, because they weren’t asked.', 'web_url': 'https://parenting.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/08/07/a-missed-opportunity-g-o-p-debate-avoids-family-policy-issues/', 'snippet': 'Do any of the Republican candidates support policies that support working families? We don’t know, because they weren’t asked.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I watched the first 2016 Republican debate with four children, aged 14, 11, 9 and 9. That meant juggling pizza off the grill while juggling questions about immigration (“But why does it matter if they come here? We’re all on the same continent.”); foreign policy (We have enemies? Who? China?); and Donald Trump’s hair. It meant introducing my oldest son to Politifact and its Pants on Fire GIF and teaching my suddenly very worried youngest daughter (“America is under attack?”) about the concept of exaggerating for dramatic affect (“just like your sister does”).', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/06/nytnow/06nytnow-debate11/06nytnow-debate11-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/08/06/nytnow/06nytnow-debate11/06nytnow-debate11-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/06/nytnow/06nytnow-debate11/06nytnow-debate11-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/06/nytnow/06nytnow-debate11/06nytnow-debate11-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1366, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/06/nytnow/06nytnow-debate11/06nytnow-debate11-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/08/06/nytnow/06nytnow-debate11/06nytnow-debate11-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/06/nytnow/06nytnow-debate11/06nytnow-debate11-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Missed Opportunity: G.O.P. Debate Avoids Family Policy Issues', 'kicker': 'Motherlode', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2015-08-07T15:16:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Styles', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': "By Kj Dell'Antonia", 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kj', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': "Dell'Antonia", 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e22233c8-f6bf-5b49-be91-f90dee0af9f5', 'word_count': 709, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e22233c8-f6bf-5b49-be91-f90dee0af9f5'}, {'abstract': 'A woman faces sudden singlehood and the loss of a confidante. ', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/08/09/books/review/lauren-foxs-days-of-awe.html', 'snippet': 'A woman faces sudden singlehood and the loss of a confidante. ', 'lead_paragraph': 'What’s wonderful about reading a novelist’s books in back-to-back sequence is that you witness the writer grow not gradually, but seemingly overnight. In Lauren Fox’s first novel, “Still Life With Husband,” an unhappily married woman who works for a journal called Male Reproduction (complete with, yes, a boss named Dick) satisfies her seven-year itch with a hunky but sensitive writer she meets in a cafe. Fox furthers these themes with a more mature voice in her delightful “Friends Like Us” — a story of relationships made all kinds of confused when the protagonist’s best friend as an adult falls for her best friend from adolescence.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/09/books/review/09PARK/09park-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 457, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/08/09/books/review/09PARK/09park-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 457}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/09/books/review/09PARK/09park-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 780, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/09/books/review/09PARK/09park-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1394, 'width': 1830, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/09/books/review/09PARK/09PARK-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/08/09/books/review/09PARK/09PARK-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/09/books/review/09PARK/09PARK-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Lauren Fox’s ‘Days of Awe’', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Pull Yourself Together', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Fox, Lauren', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Days of Awe (Book)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-08-07T15:16:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Patricia Park', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Patricia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Park', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/f1c880d3-0219-50b6-81a4-4ef0952dadd1', 'word_count': 636, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f1c880d3-0219-50b6-81a4-4ef0952dadd1'}, {'abstract': 'Paperback books of particular interest.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/08/09/books/review/paperback-row.html', 'snippet': 'Paperback books of particular interest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'PANIC IN A SUITCASE, by Yelena Akhtiorskaya. (Riverhead, $16.) The boundaries between the Old and New Worlds blur in this debut novel, which follows a Ukrainian family adjusting to Brighton Beach, Brooklyn. Spanning 15 years of their lives, the resulting story is, as Karolina Waclawiak wrote here, a “rewarding biography of displacement, where those left behind are often as disconnected as those who flee for an elusive better life elsewhere.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '31', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Paperback Row', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Paperback Row', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-08-07T15:17:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Joumana Khatib', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joumana', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Khatib', 'qualif