 'section_name': 'Your Money', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tara Siegel Bernard', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tara', 'middlename': 'Siegel', 'lastname': 'Bernard', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a9eeaeca-cbd4-5ea9-a682-e36d65e4418e', 'word_count': 1419, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a9eeaeca-cbd4-5ea9-a682-e36d65e4418e'}, {'abstract': 'Narratives of police brutality often exclude women, but the scourge of racism doesn’t belong to a single gender.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/08/07/magazine/female-visibility-matters.html', 'snippet': 'Narratives of police brutality often exclude women, but the scourge of racism doesn’t belong to a single gender.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A few weeks ago, I taught a social-justice workshop for African-American teenage girls from Chicago’s West Side. They all attend school in North Lawndale, one of the city’s most impoverished neighborhoods, and many of them have experienced hostile or violent interactions with the police. But because they are girls, not boys, they’ve told me, most people in their community do not take those interactions seriously.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/03/magazine/03mag-sandrabland-1/03mag-sandrabland-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/08/03/magazine/03mag-sandrabland-1/03mag-sandrabland-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/03/magazine/03mag-sandrabland-1/03mag-sandrabland-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': No