e': 'J.', 'lastname': 'de la Merced', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f15d15a0-a11a-5e60-b821-da0ac6e68635', 'word_count': 220, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f15d15a0-a11a-5e60-b821-da0ac6e68635'}, {'abstract': 'A battle over affordable housing, race and class in Yonkers in the 1980s offers some lessons for battles being fought in New York City.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/08/09/nyregion/a-yonkers-education.html', 'snippet': 'A battle over affordable housing, race and class in Yonkers in the 1980s offers some lessons for battles being fought in New York City.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the second hour of “Show Me a Hero,” David Simon’s long-awaited HBO miniseries about a crisis of race and class that erupted in Yonkers in the 1980s and reverberated for years, a crazed mob floods City Hall to denounce a plan, mandated by a federal court, to residentially desegregate the city. Yonkers is vertically divided by the Saw Mill River Parkway: The east side had traditionally been home to white middle-income earners, many of whom were Italian-American; the west side was poor, and black and Hispanic. In 1988, close to 98 percent of Yonkers’s public housing stock was located in the southwest section, which contained 81 percent of the city’s minority population. ', 'print_section': 'MB', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/09/nyregion/09BIG1/09BIG1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 424, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/08/09/nyregion/09BIG1/09BIG1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 424}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/09/nyregion/09BIG1/09BIG1-jumbo.jpg', 'height'