s', 'value': 'Doctors Without Borders', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Mare Nostrum', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'AFRICA', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Italy', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-08-07T20:24:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gaia Pianigiani', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gaia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pianigiani', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/575dd693-05af-50e6-b737-57c2f58b93f2', 'word_count': 776, 'uri': 'nyt://article/575dd693-05af-50e6-b737-57c2f58b93f2'}, {'abstract': 'Mixing street smarts, activism and her Muslim identity, Linda Sarsour has become a political force and is setting her sights on New York’s City Council.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/08/09/nyregion/linda-sarsour-is-a-brooklyn-homegirl-in-a-hijab.html', 'snippet': 'Mixing street smarts, activism and her Muslim identity, Linda Sarsour has become a political force and is setting her sights on New York’s City Council.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Behind schedule as usual, Linda Sarsour rushed into the Times Square office of the civil rights group the Gathering for Justice last month, 40 minutes late for a meeting with its founder, Harry Belafonte. On the way in from Brooklyn, the Uber driver she had hired made a wrong turn and wound up in New Jersey. Now, wearing her head scarf and hungry from fasting for Ramadan, Ms. Sarsour scurried into an auditorium packed with some of the city’s most prominent social-justice warriors.', 'print_section': 'MB', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/09/nyregion/09SARSOU