major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-08-07T21:10:30+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'T Magazine', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By JOHN WOGAN', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'WOGAN', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interactive Feature', '_id': 'nyt://interactive/ea72ba1e-0b5b-5eea-8b0d-d1a0a4601e17', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://interactive/ea72ba1e-0b5b-5eea-8b0d-d1a0a4601e17'}, {'abstract': 'The Film Society of Lincoln Center screens the action-adventure movies that star Michael York, Oliver Reed, Faye Dunaway and Charlton Heston.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/08/09/movies/the-three-musketeers-the-richard-lester-treatment.html', 'snippet': 'The Film Society of Lincoln Center screens the action-adventure movies that star Michael York, Oliver Reed, Faye Dunaway and Charlton Heston.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Countless directors have taken on Alexandre Dumas’s “The Three Musketeers,” but none have matched Richard Lester’s adaptation, so rambunctious it took “The Three Musketeers” (1973) and “The Four Musketeers” (1974) to contain it. The Film Society at Lincoln Center screens both Sunday and Tuesday. (Also on Sunday: Mr. Lester’s “The Return of the Musketeers,” from 1989.) The novel’s action and humor play to Mr. Lester’s gifts for the same, and working from George Macdonald Fraser’s script, the director attracted an unsurpassable cast, including Faye Dunaway as the lethal Milady de Winter; Christopher Lee as an eyepatched Rochefort; Raquel Welch as Constance; Michael York as D’Artagnan; Oliver Reed as Athos; and a peerless Charlton Heston as Cardinal Richelieu. ', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/09/arts/09GUIDE6/09GUIDE6-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/08/09/arts/09GUIDE6/09GUIDE6-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/09/arts/09GUIDE6/09GUIDE6-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/09/arts/09GUIDE6/09GUIDE6-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1367, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/09/arts/09GUIDE6/09GUIDE6-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/08/09/arts/09GUIDE6/09GUIDE6-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/09/arts/09GUIDE6/09GUIDE6-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘The Three Musketeers,’ the Richard Lester Treatment', 'kicker': 'The Week Ahead', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Film; Up to the Hilt in Swashbucklers', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Three Musketeers (Movie)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Four Musketeers (Movie)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Return of the Musketeers (Movie)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-08-07T21:11:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andy Webster', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Webster', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1a730cc1-90a9-5fee-b559-1b82cd239a49', 'word_count': 154, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1a730cc1-90a9-5fee-b559-1b82cd239a49'}, {'abstract': 'Mr. Silver works out at the gym, takes his camera to various parks to survey how the city’s public spaces are being used, and spends time with his family and on social media.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/08/09/nyregion/how-mitchell-j-silver-new-york-city-parks-commissioner-spends-his-sundays.html', 'snippet': 'Mr. Silver works out at the gym, takes his camera to various parks to survey how the city’s public spaces are being used, and spends time with his family and on social media.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A lot of New Yorkers spend their weekends frolicking in the city’s 1,700 parks, but Mitchell J. Silver takes his visits more seriously than most. As the New York City parks commissioner, Mr. Silver picks a different borough every Sunday, packs his beloved camera and sets off to observe and document how a select few parks are being used and how they could be improved. A born-and-bred New Yorker, the 55-year-old urban planner recently returned to the city after serving for nearly a decade in North Carolina as the planning director for its capital city, Raleigh. Mr. Silver lives with his wife, Mary, 48, an administrator for the nonprofit Brooklyn Community Services, and his daughter, Rachel, 17, in Downtown Brooklyn.', 'print_section': 'MB', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/09/nyregion/09ROUTINE1/09ROUTINE1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/08/09/nyregion/09ROUTINE1/09ROUTINE1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/09/nyregion/09ROUTINE1/09ROUTINE1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/09/nyregion/09ROUTINE1/09ROUTINE1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/09/nyregion/09ROUTINE1/09ROUTINE1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/08/09/nyregion/09ROUTINE1/09ROUTINE1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/09/nyregion/09ROUTINE1/09ROUTINE1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'How Mitchell J. Silver, New York City Parks Commissioner, Spends His Sundays', 'kicker': 'Sunday Routine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Walk in the Parks', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Silver, Mitchell J', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Parks and Recreation Department (NYC)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parks and Other Recreation Areas', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-08-07T21:14:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lisa W. Foderaro', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lisa', 'middlename': 'W.', 'lastname': 'Foderaro', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/781fb525-90ec-59a6-a490-7d4d59fd8234', 'word_count': 803, 'uri': 'nyt://article/781fb525-90ec-59a6-a490-7d4d59fd8234'}, {'abstract': 'Founded in 1999, the publishing arm of The New York Review of Books has reissued everything from ancient Chinese poems to a never-released Chekhov story collection to the literary sleeper “Stoner.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/08/08/books/new-york-review-books-fills-a-niche-by-reviving-forgotten-works.html', 'snippet': 'Founded in 1999, the publishing arm of The New York Review of Books has reissued everything from ancient Chinese poems to a never-released Chekhov story collection to the literary sleeper “Stoner.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'In 1882, the writer Anton Chekhov, just 22 and beginning his literary career, submitted a dozen short stories to Czar Alexander III’s censors, who discerned a subversive intent and promptly forbade publication of the collection, called “The Prank.” The satirical tales then languished in official archives for more than 130 years — until last week, when they were finally published, in English, by New York Review Books.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/08/arts/08NYRB/08NYRB-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 392, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/08/08/arts/08NYRB/08NYRB-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 392}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 