jor': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-08-07T22:05:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Styles', 'section_name': 'Fashion & Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bill Cunningham', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bill', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cunningham', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d85198c6-f8ed-552a-9460-07326ef277a0', 'word_count': 24, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d85198c6-f8ed-552a-9460-07326ef277a0'}, {'abstract': 'The male warbler sports a black, broad bandit’s mask, and male and female may be the most beautiful birds no one knows.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/08/07/common-yellowthroat-warbler-a-bandit-in-the-bramble/', 'snippet': 'The male warbler sports a black, broad bandit’s mask, and male and female may be the most beautiful birds no one knows.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Though tiny, the common yellowthroat warbler is no church mouse. Unlike many of their beautiful warbler relatives, these birds shun the treetops for a life amid the tangled brambles and vines of wetland edges and dense thickets. Highly inquisitive — even aggressive while protecting a breeding territory — the birds are animated subjects for anyone with even a passing interest in bird-watching. A lengthy view of the three- to four-inch warbler is measured in seconds.', 'print_section': 'MB', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/09/nyregion/09NATURE/09NATURE-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 543, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/08/09/nyregion/09NATURE/09NATURE-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 543}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'c