, 'value': 'Civilian Casualties', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-08-08T18:33:25+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'World', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Middle East', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/72093bc1-5fbe-5553-a0ce-2394d02eeedb', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/72093bc1-5fbe-5553-a0ce-2394d02eeedb'}, {'abstract': 'A Houston anti-poverty program tries something radical: Ask people what they want, then give it to them.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/08/09/opinion/sunday/david-l-kirp-what-do-the-poor-need-try-asking-them.html', 'snippet': 'A Houston anti-poverty program tries something radical: Ask people what they want, then give it to them.', 'lead_paragraph': 'FOR decades, policy makers have treated poverty as a sign of helplessness and ineptitude. The worse off the neighborhood — the higher the rate of poverty, crime, and juvenile delinquency — the less influence it would have over its future. Social service agencies conducted “needs assessments” rather than asking residents what would strengthen their community. Government agencies or private entrepreneurs then delivered brick-and-mortar solutions — a new school, medical clinic or housing.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/09/opinion/sunday/09kirp/09kirp-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 378, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/08/09/opinion/sunday/09kirp/09kirp-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 378}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/09/opinion/sunday/09kirp/09kirp-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 645, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/09/opinion/sunday/09kirp/09kirp-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1291, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/09/opinion/sunday/09kirp/09kirp-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/08/09/opinion/sunday/09kirp/09kirp-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/09/opinion/sunday/09kirp/09kirp-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'What Do the Poor Need? Try Asking Them', 'kicker': 'Contributing Op-Ed Writer', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Houston, We Have a Solution', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kirp, David L', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Poverty', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Houston (Tex)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Neighborhood Centers Inc', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-08-08T18:34:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By David L. Kirp', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': 'L.', 'lastname': 'Kirp', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d4dbc94c-f224-58e9-b0ce-d9df252769c5', 'word_count': 1208, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d4dbc94c-f224-58e9-b0ce-d9df252769c5'}, {'abstract': 'President Joseph Kabila is carving the country’s 11 provinces into 26, which many Congolese see as a ploy to delay the coming presidential election and allow Mr. Kabila to “slide” into a third term.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/08/09/world/africa/congolese-see-no-end-to-presidents-final-term.html', 'snippet': 'President Joseph Kabila is carving the country’s 11 provinces into 26, which many Congolese see as a ploy to delay the coming presidential election and allow Mr. Kabila to “slide” into a third term.', 'lead_paragraph': 'KINSHASA, Democratic Republic of Congo — To take the political pulse of this sprawling, mineral-rich country, head to the busiest stretch of Lumumba Boulevard, one of the capital’s main roads. On weekdays, a large crowd gathers here to peer at the newspapers posted on an eight-foot-high wall and loudly trade opinions on the news of the day.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Congolese See No End to President’s Final Term ', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Congolese See No End to Leader’s Final Term', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Congo, Democratic Republic of (Congo-Kinshasa)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kabila, Joseph', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Demonstrations, Protests and Riots', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elections', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-08-08T18:41:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Africa', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sarah M. Kazadi and Rebecca Sesny', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sarah', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt:/