d', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Waqar', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gillani', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Salman', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Masood', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b30edff5-dc6e-59ea-8b5f-e875396559bd', 'word_count': 772, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b30edff5-dc6e-59ea-8b5f-e875396559bd'}, {'abstract': 'The rule is the latest in a series of reforms Gov. Andrew M. Cuomo has introduced to address worker exploitation in nail salons.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/08/10/nyregion/new-york-combating-worker-abuse-will-require-salon-owners-to-secure-wage-bonds.html', 'snippet': 'The rule is the latest in a series of reforms Gov. Andrew M. Cuomo has introduced to address worker exploitation in nail salons.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For nearly six years, Silvia Gonzalez opened the doors of Romy’s Nails in Brooklyn at about 8:45 a.m. and left more than 12 hours later, six days a week. She performed manicures and pedicures with no lunch break, making $55 a day, or about $4.50 an hour. After Ms. Gonzalez had worked there four years, her pay increased to $80 a day, which was still below minimum wage. She recalled hearing her employer often proclaim to workers, many of whom were working illegally in the country, that no one could win against him in court.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/10/nyregion/10NAILSweb/10NAILSweb-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/08/10/nyregion/10NAILSweb/10NAILSweb-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None,