': 'organizations', 'value': 'AL QAEDA', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Defense Department', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-08-10T17:17:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Middle East', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Hubbard', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hubbard', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8ddba0a9-e2aa-55a8-bee1-9e391c06bc68', 'word_count': 546, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8ddba0a9-e2aa-55a8-bee1-9e391c06bc68'}, {'abstract': 'A limited-edition spirit from Tom Nichol; Creemee, a soft-serve Vermont-style ice cream; Worcestershire sauce with a lager base; and more.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/08/12/dining/tanqueray-gin-bloomsbury.html', 'snippet': 'A limited-edition spirit from Tom Nichol; Creemee, a soft-serve Vermont-style ice cream; Worcestershire sauce with a lager base; and more.', 'lead_paragraph': 'An unmistakable whiff of juniper rises from the glass holding the latest iteration of Tanqueray gin. Called Bloomsbury, the gin is named after the location of the company’s English distillery in the late 19th century. It is a limited-edition spirit that the company’s former distiller Tom Nichol based on a recipe created by Charles Waugh Tanqueray. Though it is in the bracing style of London dry gin, it also has spicy, licorice and floral notes. Sip it on the rocks with a subtle touch of lime, or let it loose in a gimlet or a southside: Tanqueray Bloomsbury gin, $33 at reservebar.com.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/12/dining/12Burner1/12Burner1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 900, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/08/12/dining/12Burner1/12Burner1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 900}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/12/dining/12Burner1/12Burner1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 683, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/12/dining/12Burner1/12Burner1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1366, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/12/dining/12Burner1/12Burner1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/08/12/dining/12Burner1/12Burner1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/12/dining/12Burner1/12Burner1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Tanqueray Gin With a Past', 'kicker': 'Front Burner', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Oysters', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Food', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cooking and Cookbooks', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Gin (Liquor)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ice Cream and Frozen Desserts', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ricker, Andy', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Nichol, Thomas', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Worcestershire Sauce', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-08-10T17:28:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Dining', 'section_name': 'Food', 'byline': {'original': 'By Florence Fabricant', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Florence', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fabricant', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d5a6ea66-ffd9-5bbe-a833-3eef63226080', 'word_count': 727, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d5a6ea66-ffd9-5bbe-a833-3eef63226080'}, {'abstract': 'A cop searches for his ex-wife, a haunted Vietnamese immigrant, in the city’s sleazy depths.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/08/16/books/review/vu-trans-dragonfish.html', 'snippet': 'A cop searches for his ex-wife, a haunted Vietnamese immigrant, in the city’s sleazy depths.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Contrary to all protestations, America is a nation not of immigrants but of refugees. Trauma, displacement and fanatical hope have marked all new Americans from the occupants of the Mayflower through every subsequent group who came to these shores (or who were brought here by force). This is the unspoken and sometimes unacknowledged fear and fact of being American: These unkind ghosts of our pasts, these specters of previous selves and previous nations that will not be dismissed so easily, always attend our daily negotiations around identity. They cause a torment that can turn us violent, hateful and self-destructive, and the uneasy grace we win is in itself a remarkable thing. The longer our stay in the United States, the fainter the ghosts get, until they are not much more than a vague unsettled feeling that itself causes even more confusion. Novels by recently arrived Americans have tried to negotiate the struggle to fit into a new home that doesn’t always want them, and the nostalgia for all that has been lost. In this way new immigrant literature mimics its antecedents.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/16/books/review/16ABANI/16ABANI-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 246, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/08/16/books/review/16ABANI/16ABANI-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 246}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/16/books/review/16ABANI/16ABANI-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 420, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/16/books/review/16ABANI/16ABANI-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 839, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/16/books/review/16ABANI/16ABANI-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/08/16/books/review/16ABANI/16ABANI-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/16/books/review/16ABANI/16ABANI-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Vu Tran’s ‘Dragonfish’', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'What Happens in Vegas', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tran, Vu (1975- )', 'rank': 