is as part of the commemoration of Michael Brown’s killing last year. In the middle of the frame was a famous face: that of Cornel West, the prominent activist and academic. But the caption, a single sentence, was general and did not identify him.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Perfectly Reasonable Question: Why Not Identify Cornel West in Front-Page Caption?', 'kicker': 'The Public Editor&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2015-08-12T12:00:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Margaret Sullivan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Margaret', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sullivan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2b0b4439-6cdd-5bae-8229-e9484c249e58', 'word_count': 411, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2b0b4439-6cdd-5bae-8229-e9484c249e58'}, {'abstract': 'After a series of Twitter posts in response to a Vanity Fair article on dating and technology, Tinder acknowledged that it overreacted.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/08/13/world/asia/tinders-claim-of-many-users-in-north-korea-meets-mockery-online.html', 'snippet': 'After a series of Twitter posts in response to a Vanity Fair article on dating and technology, Tinder acknowledged that it overreacted.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Like a person scorned after a bad date, the tech company Tinder went a little bit crazy on social media on Tuesday after Vanity Fair published an article blaming technology for the death of dating.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/12/business/12tinder2/12tinder2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articl