'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-08-12T14:44:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Uki Goñi', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Uki', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Goñi', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/29142ec1-576b-5992-b15f-e4233853976f', 'word_count': 924, 'uri': 'nyt://article/29142ec1-576b-5992-b15f-e4233853976f'}, {'abstract': 'In a move unthinkable not long ago, Parliament has voted to erect a statue of João Bernardo Vieira, a tempestuous leader whose killing is unsolved.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/08/13/world/africa/guinea-bissau-autocrat-6-years-after-his-brutal-murder-makes-comeback.html', 'snippet': 'In a move unthinkable not long ago, Parliament has voted to erect a statue of João Bernardo Vieira, a tempestuous leader whose killing is unsolved.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BISSAU, Guinea-Bissau — A mystery has hung over this sweltering West African capital for six years, but not because no one could solve it. Many people have an answer, though they will not say so in public: Who killed Nino?', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/12/world/bissau-web/bisseu-web-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 421, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/08/12/world/bissau-web/bisseu-web-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 421}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/12/world/bissau-web/bisseu-web-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 719, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type':