o prosecute a law enforcement officer in the death of a member of the public.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The state has banned the use of grand juries to determine whether to prosecute a law enforcement officer in the death of a member of the public. The law, which Gov. Jerry Brown signed on Tuesday, comes a year after the death of Michael Brown by a police officer in Ferguson, Mo., sparked nationwide protests. A grand jury in Missouri did not indict the officer, Darren Wilson. Experts said that the use of grand juries in criminal cases already was relatively rare in California. But now prosecutors will no longer have the option of sending difficult cases to grand juries: they will have to decide whether to indict police officers. Because grand jury testimony is often held in secret, Holly Mitchell, the author of the law, said that the change would increase trust in police officers and transparency in government. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'California: Grand Juries Are Barred From Deciding Cases Involving Police ', 'kicker': 'National Briefing | West', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'California: Grand Juries Are Barred From Deciding Cases Involving Police ', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'California', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Jury System', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Police Brutality, Misconduct and Shootings', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Brown, Edmund G Jr', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-08-13T03:26:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ian Lovett', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ian', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lovett', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/873ee5ae-c649-5a24-b601-32b4af9f6e61', 'word_count': 140, 'uri': 'nyt://article/873ee5ae-c649-5a24-b601-32b4af9f6e61'}, {'abstract': 'Kale and Aubry Walch started the line of small-batch meat alternatives last year.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/08/13/t-magazine/meat-without-meat-courtesy-of-herbivorous-butcher.html', 'snippet': 'Kale and Aubry Walch started the line of small-batch meat alternatives last year.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Kale and Aubry Walch launched the Herbivorous Butcher, a line of small-batch meat alternatives, last year, after recognizing a demand for vegan food that doesn’t taste like vegan food. When the brother-sister duo from Minneapolis, Minn., first brought their locally sourced, protein-rich products — smoky BBQ ribs, maple-glazed bacon, spicy chorizo, all made from creative blends of wheat gluten, beans and flavorings — to the local farmer’s market, they caused a small sensation. “One person said, ‘I’m a food scientist, and I swear I can taste fat marbling in that sausage,’\u2009” recalls Aubry. After appearing at a series of pop-ups across the Midwest, they’re now opening a brick-and-mortar location in their hometown. Says the aptly named Kale, “Our goal is to fool people into saving the planet.”', 'print_section': 'M2', 'print_page': '126', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/23/t-magazine/23womens-look-butcher/23womens-look-butcher-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/08/23/t-magazine/23womens-look-butcher/23womens-look-butcher-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/23/t-magazine/23womens-look-butcher/23womens-look-butcher-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 667, 'width':