ine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jenna Wortham', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jenna', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wortham', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0b397b2a-c266-5fe7-8e46-8b84182ebf51', 'word_count': 384, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0b397b2a-c266-5fe7-8e46-8b84182ebf51'}, {'abstract': 'Diving boards began disappearing from New York City’s public pools in the 1970s amid safety concerns. Now only three remain in regular use, calling to the adventurous.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/08/16/nyregion/bounce-launch-splash-the-joy-of-the-diving-board.html', 'snippet': 'Diving boards began disappearing from New York City’s public pools in the 1970s amid safety concerns. Now only three remain in regular use, calling to the adventurous.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Warren Davis, 18, had trekked over from Avenue D for this moment, and he made the most of it. After a preliminary bounce on the diving board at the pool at the Tony Dapolito Recreation Center, he took three steps, paused and started his signature move, a lofty dive with a midflight spin that ended with his slicing effortlessly into the water.', 'print_section': 'MB', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/16/nyregion/16DIVECOMBO1/16DIVECOMBO1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 192, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/08/16/nyregion/16DIVECOMBO1/16DIVECOMBO1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 192}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/16/nyregion/16DIVECOMBO1/16DIVECOMBO1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 328, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 