{'original': 'By Emily Steel', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Emily', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Steel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b7cf27d3-ad02-522c-9702-a1266ca926c8', 'word_count': 176, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b7cf27d3-ad02-522c-9702-a1266ca926c8'}, {'abstract': 'The director discusses a sequence from the film featuring Henry Cavill, Armie Hammer and Alicia Vikander.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/08/14/movies/the-man-from-uncle-with-movie-trailer-guy-ritchie-narrates-a-scene.html', 'snippet': 'The director discusses a sequence from the film featuring Henry Cavill, Armie Hammer and Alicia Vikander.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The 1960s television spy series “The Man From U.N.C.L.E.” gets a big screen update from the director Guy Ritchie. Henry Cavill and Armie Hammer take the lead roles of Napoleon Solo and Illya Kuryakin, CIA and KGB agents forced to join forces, with an assist from Alicia Vikander. This early scene pits the two against each another in a divided Berlin. In an interview, Mr. Ritchie spoke about the sequence and ideas for the movie. Following are excerpts from that conversation.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Man From U.N.C.L.E. (With Movie Trailer): Guy Ritchie Narrates a Scene', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ritchie, Guy', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-08-13T16:39:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mekado Murphy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mekado', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Murphy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2a3ef518-fdad-5c14-ac06-3875f782f92f', 'word_count': 369, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2a3ef518-fdad-5c14-ac06-3875f782f92f'}, {'abstract': 'Guy Ritchie’s big-screen rendering of the 1960s television series tries hard not to be a museum piece, partly with a slick, agitated style.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/08/14/movies/review-the-man-from-uncle-resurrects-a-glossy-action-packed-60s.html', 'snippet': 'Guy Ritchie’s big-screen rendering of the 1960s television series tries hard not to be a museum piece, partly with a slick, agitated style.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Guy Ritchie makes the kind of enjoyably disreputable movies that are fun to watch until they’re not. He’s a talented flimflam artist and, for the industry, I imagine, a useful one because of how he glosses up schlocky, sketchy projects (his “Sherlock Holmes” movies and other hyperkinetic baubles), making them seem as if there’s more to their slick surfaces than naked commercialism, agency fees and facile pleasure. It works for him (he keeps getting hired), and sometimes also for us. Pleasure is, after all, rarely overrated, including in the often mind-bludgeoning arena of franchise cinema, and there’s a lot to be said for watching beautiful people doing very silly stuff on screen. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/14/arts/14MAN/14MAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 399, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/08/14/arts/14MAN/14MAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 399}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/14/arts/14MAN/14MAN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 