)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-08-13T22:00:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stephen Holden', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stephen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Holden', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/de874fe8-b9a5-584c-ae73-b0efa3073da1', 'word_count': 427, 'uri': 'nyt://article/de874fe8-b9a5-584c-ae73-b0efa3073da1'}, {'abstract': 'In the sleepy surf town of Bellport, eight young creatives have launched an interdisciplinary space in a vacant auto body shop.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/08/13/long-island-artists-collective-auto-body-bellport/', 'snippet': 'In the sleepy surf town of Bellport, eight young creatives have launched an interdisciplinary space in a vacant auto body shop.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The common good may seem like an elusive political ideal — or a naive one. But for a group of eight young creatives on Long Island, banding together to form a collective to promote the arts has revitalized a small, sleepy surf town. Auto Body, as both the crew and its community space are known, formed last summer when sisters Georgia and Claire Read (whose father, Howard, founded the blue-chip gallery Cheim & Read) and their photographer friend Johnny Knapp “pushed our way,” as Claire puts it, into a vacant auto body shop (hence the name) in Bellport, N.Y. “The space came first, in a way,” she explains. “We knew it had been vacant for years and years, and we wanted to exhibit art and design and hold events, create a space for everyone in the village, bring a creative energy to Main Street,\xa0because it was lacking before.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/13/t-magazine/13onview-slide-F74O/13onv