': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5decedbc-3a3d-552f-8f77-998378cda4de', 'word_count': 1266, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5decedbc-3a3d-552f-8f77-998378cda4de'}, {'abstract': 'The United States is being more pragmatic in its approach in Latin America, and it’s paying off.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/08/14/opinion/in-latin-america-quiet-diplomacy-bears-fruit.html', 'snippet': 'The United States is being more pragmatic in its approach in Latin America, and it’s paying off.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Nearly seven years ago, President Evo Morales of Bolivia expelled the American ambassador, denouncing him for “conspiring against democracy and seeking the division of Bolivia.” Months later, Mr. Morales’s government kicked the Drug Enforcement Administration out of La Paz, in a rebuke of Washington’s approach to counternarcotics in the region. In 2013, Bolivia ejected the American government’s international aid agency, which Mr. Morales accused of working secretly to destabilize his government.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '24', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/14/opinion/14fri2/14fri2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 742, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/08/14/opinion/14fri2/14fri2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 742}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/14/opinion/14fri2/14fri2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 828, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/14/opinion/14fri2/14fri2-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1713, 'width': 1385, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': '