ection_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By Katie Rogers', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Katie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rogers', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0045cf8a-f22e-5ed1-bf39-ae9c132ba702', 'word_count': 441, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0045cf8a-f22e-5ed1-bf39-ae9c132ba702'}, {'abstract': 'The state’s Republican-dominated Legislature was forced back into a special session this week to make a third attempt at drawing congressional boundaries.', 'web_url': 'https://takingnote.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/08/14/florida-legislators-deny-gerrymandering-as-they-slouch-back-to-the-drawing-board/', 'snippet': 'The state’s Republican-dominated Legislature was forced back into a special session this week to make a third attempt at drawing congressional boundaries.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In 2010, 63 percent of Florida voters approved a “Fair Districts” amendment to the state constitution designed to end the partisan dirty pool that is as old as Congress itself — the gerrymandering of congressional boundaries to favor one party over the other. Patrick Henry tried it first in Virginia in 1788, having lines drawn to block James Madison from a congressional seat, but Madison prevailed before the voters.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/14/opinion/14clinesWEB/14clinesWEB-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 419, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/08/14/opinion/14clinesWEB/14clinesWEB-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 419}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/14/opinion/14clinesWEB/14clinesWEB-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 716, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'cr