ons', 'value': 'Florida', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-08-14T14:27:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Francis X. Clines', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Francis', 'middlename': 'X.', 'lastname': 'Clines', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ea12019b-d192-59b4-a659-3c62309e4aac', 'word_count': 324, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ea12019b-d192-59b4-a659-3c62309e4aac'}, {'abstract': 'From the International Herald Tribune archives: The unoccupied zone in France gets its own guillotine executioner in 1940.', 'web_url': 'https://iht-retrospective.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/08/14/1940-france-gets-a-2nd-guillotine/', 'snippet': 'From the International Herald Tribune archives: The unoccupied zone in France gets its own guillotine executioner in 1940.', 'lead_paragraph': 'VICHY — The partition of France into two zones, occupied and unoccupied, has had the unfortunate consequence of depriving the French government of the services of the official executioner, known as ‘‘Monsieur Paris,’’ who is in Paris. So the civil authorities have had to call on ‘‘Monsieur Algeria,’’ who in civilian life goes by the name of Henri Roch, to behead criminals guilty of capital offenses. ‘‘Monsieur Algeria’’ has arrived in Nimes from North Africa with three assistants and a guillotine. There he will discharge the duties of his gruesome office until the French government is permitted by the Germans to return to Paris. — New York Herald Tribune, August 15, 1940', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '1940: France Gets a 2nd Guillotine', 'kicker': 'IHT Retrospective', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Capital Punishment', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'World War II (1939-45)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'France', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-08-14T14:30:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By International Herald Tribune', 'person': [{'firstname': 'International', 'middlename': 'Herald', 'lastname': 'Tribune', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0e2ffd83-656f-56f1-b40f-646700098f98', 'word_count': 112, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0e2ffd83-656f-56f1-b40f-646700098f98'}, {'abstract': 'A judge in London reduced bail for Navinder Singh Sarao, who has been in custody since April and is fighting extradition to the United States.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/08/15/business/dealbook/bail-reduced-for-british-trader-charged-in-flash-crash.html', 'snippet': 'A judge in London reduced bail for Navinder Singh Sarao, who has been in custody since April and is fighting extradition to the United States.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LONDON — Navinder Singh Sarao, the British futures trader charged by United States authorities with contributing to a sudden plunge in American stock markets five years ago, was assigned a reduced bail at a court hearing on Friday, allowing him to be released from custody.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/15/business/15db-flash-web/15db-flash-web-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 379, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/08/15/business/15db-flash-web/15db-flash-web-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 379}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/15/business/15db-flash-web/15db-flash-web-jumbo.jpg', 'h