 'value': "Lincoln's Billy (Book)", 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-08-14T14:49:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kyla Wazana Tompkins', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kyla', 'middlename': 'Wazana', 'lastname': 'Tompkins', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c5b4d357-8abf-5a6b-853c-a98ec3be3d03', 'word_count': 952, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c5b4d357-8abf-5a6b-853c-a98ec3be3d03'}, {'abstract': 'A homecoming coincides with a Cuban boy’s rescue at sea. ', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/08/16/books/review/make-your-home-among-strangers-by-jennine-capo-crucet.html', 'snippet': 'A homecoming coincides with a Cuban boy’s rescue at sea. ', 'lead_paragraph': 'Elián González, the 6-year-old boy at the heated center of the international custody battle that tore apart Miami and the Cuban-American community in 2000, has resurfaced in the news. Fifteen years after the predawn raid by American agents that led to his return to his father in Cuba, he has announced that he would like to visit the United States again. With the thaw in relations between the two countries, the times are vastly different. But as we are expansively reminded in “Make Your Home Among Strangers,” Jennine Capó Crucet’s heartfelt first novel about a Cuban-American family entangled in a fictionalized account of the González affair, no amount of change erases the past. With history onboard, Crucet shows us how journeys between cultures are almost impossible to navigate and family relationships are bound to dump us in choppy waters.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/16/books/review/16MA/16MA-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 819, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/08/16/books/review/16MA/16MA-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 819}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/16/books/review/16MA/16MA-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 750, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/16/books/review/16MA/16MA-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1500, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/16/books/review/16MA/16MA-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/08/16/books/review/16MA/16MA-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/16/books/review/16MA/16MA-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘Make Your Home Among Strangers,’ by Jennine Capó Crucet', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Tossed About', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Crucet, Jennine Capo (1981- )', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Make Your Home Among Strangers (Book)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-08-14T14:50:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kathryn Ma', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kathryn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ma', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/966fa3e8-3370-56bc-89ff-396412ee4a06', 'word_count': 611, 'uri': 'nyt://article/966fa3e8-3370-56bc-89ff-396412ee4a06'}, {'abstract': 'In his first novel, the playwright Dario Fo depicts a Lucrezia Borgia who belies the rumors.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/08/16/books/review/the-popes-daughter-by-dario-fo.html', 'snippet': 'In his first novel, the playwright Dario Fo depicts a Lucrezia Borgia who belies the rumors.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Last year, at the venerable age of 88, the Italian comic Dario Fo took on a new creative challenge. The veteran of more than 60 years as an actor, painter, director, stage designer and playwright (as well as the recipient of the 1997 Nobel Prize in Literature), he had in recent years begun to deliver delightful public lectures on artists — Giotto, Leonardo, Raphael, Caravaggio, Picasso — and architecture, including the Romanesque cathedral of Modena. But up to 2014, he had never written a novel. That changed with “The Pope’s Daughter,” now translated into English, although needless to say for this wild man of the theater, the word “novel” is not quite enough to encompass his lightly fictional labor of love for, of all people, Lucrezia Borgia. “The Pope’s Daughter” is also a picture book, illustrated with some of Fo’s own colorful paintings, most based on Renaissance originals. It has since become a full-blown theatrical script. (Meanwhile, Fo has finished a second illustrated novel, inspired by an 18th-century Danish prince, Christian VII.)', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/16/books/review/0816-BKS-Rowland/0816-BKS-Rowland-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 497, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/08/16/books/review/0816-BKS-Rowland/0816-BKS-Rowland-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 497}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/16/books/review/0816-BKS-Rowland/0816-BKS-Rowland-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 848, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/16/books/review/0816-BKS-Rowland/0816-BKS-Rowland-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1695, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/16/books/review/0816-BKS-Rowland/0816-BKS-Rowland-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/08/16/books/review/0816-BKS-Rowland/0816-BKS-Rowland-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/16/books/review/0816-BKS-Rowland/0816-BKS-Rowland-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘The Pope’s Daughter,’ by Dario Fo', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Renaissance Woman', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Fo, Dario', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Shugaar, Antony', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': "The Pope's Daughter (Book)", 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-08-14T14:52:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ingrid Rowland', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ingrid', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rowland', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/44f23c10-cab9-516b-9eca-96c7ea4eafd7', 'word_count': 1395, 'uri': 'nyt://article/44f23c10-cab9-516b-9eca-96c7ea4eafd7'}, {'abstract': 'A novel inspired by the painter Camille Pissarro’s parents.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/08/16/books/review/the-marriage-of-opposites-by-alice-hoffman.html', 'snippet': 'A novel inspired by the painter Camille Pissarro’s parents.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For a period of her young life, Virginia Woolf wrote while standing at an elevated desk poised near her sister Vanessa’s easel. The writing table’s curious height enabled Virginia to peer at the world from the same viewpoint as her artist sister, to witness all at once the world itself, the world transcribed in ink and the world brushed onto a canvas. Later, when Woolf created the character of the painter Lily Briscoe in “To the Lighthouse,” she knew the mix of delicacy and passion that collide to create a great work, and she was schooled in the fine craft of transforming daubs of paint into strings of words.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘The Marriage of Opposites,’ by Alice Hoffman', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Mother’s Disapproval', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hoffman, Alice', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Pissarro, Camille', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Marriage of Opposites (Book)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-08-14T14:52:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Hillary Kelly', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Hillary', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kelly', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/7b29f847-8428-550a-8867-f8f46987256a', 'word_count': 783, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7b29f847-8428-550a-8867-f8f46987256a'}, {'abstract': 'After a little girl’s remains were found eight weeks ago on a Boston Harbor shoreline, the police still have few clues about who she is and how she died.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/08/15/us/mystery-of-baby-doe-troubles-massachusetts-investigators.html', 'snippet': 'After a little girl’s remains were found eight weeks ago on a Boston Harbor shoreline, the police still have few clues about who she is and how she died.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For nearly eight weeks, investigators in Massachusetts have been struggling to solve a mystery: the identity of a little girl whose remains were found on a Boston Harbor shoreline.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/15/us/15doe2_watch/15doe2_watch-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/08/15/us/15doe2_watch/15doe2_watch-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/15/us/15doe2_watch/15doe2_watch-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/15/us/15doe2_watch/15doe2_watch-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/15/us/15doe2_watch/15doe2_watch-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/08/15/us/15doe2_watch/15doe2_watch-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/15/us/15doe2_watch/15doe2_watch-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Mystery of ‘Baby Doe’ Troubles Massachusetts Investigators', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Massachusetts', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Missing Persons', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Murders, Attempted Murders and Homicides', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Boston (Mass)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Boston Harbor (Mass)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-08-14T14:52:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Christine Hauser', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Christine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hauser', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ab20f2a9-7c6c-5918-ae81-d374471a211a', 'word_count': 872, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ab20f2a9-7c6c-5918-ae81-d374471a211a'}, {'abstract': 'Two novels set in Soviet Russia exalt everyday joys and imagine Catherine the Great’s yearnings.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/08/16/books/review/brief-loves-that-live-forever-and-a-woman-loved-by-andrei-makine.html', 'snippet': 'Two novels set in Soviet Russia exalt everyday joys and imagine Catherine the Great’s yearnings.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As a child growing up in a Soviet orphanage, the narrator of Andrei Makine’s memoiristic novel “Brief Loves That Live Forever” recalls being completely in thrall to the idea of a “dazzlingly white city” where everything would be abundant and free, and people would help themselves only to as much as they needed. The harsh realities of daily life under Communism being what they are, disillusionment sets in quickly. Even so, the novel’s dreamy and impressionistic linked episodes, which take the narrator from his boyhood under Brezhnev to his adulthood, offer up this essential truth: Fleeting moments of beauty and love are to be treasured above all else. Carved out of even the most dreary and oppressive circumstances, they’re the sum of a life, a means of triumphing over brutality, a more powerful form of resistance than “dissident whisperings.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/16/books/review/16LIVSHIN/16LIVSHIN-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 882, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/08/16/books/review/16LIVSHIN/16LIVSHIN-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 882}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/16/books/review/16LIVSHIN/16LIVSHIN-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 697, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/16/books/review/16LIVSHIN/16LIVSHIN-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1750, 'width': 1191, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/16/books/review/16LIVSHIN/16LIVSHIN-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/08/16/books/review/16LIVSHIN/16LIVSHIN-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/16/books/review/16LIVSHIN/16LIVSHIN-thumbLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘Brief Loves That Live Forever’ and ‘A Woman Loved,’ by Andrei Makine', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Beauty Behind the Curtain', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Makine, Andrei', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Brief Loves That Live Forever (Book)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'A Woman Loved (Book)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-08-14T14:53:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Julia Livshin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Julia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Livshin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/5ad9dac2-8825-56eb-bf72-6212041f9174', 'word_count': 1221, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5ad9dac2-8825-56eb-bf72-6212041f9174'}, {'abstract': 'A crossword creator and con man eludes the woman who loves him, until he realizes his error.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/08/16/books/review/jeff-bartschs-two-across.html', 'snippet': 'A crossword creator and con man eludes the woman who loves him, until he realizes his error.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Right before college-age Vera Baxter disappears from Stanley Owens’s life for the second time, she wakes up in the middle of the night, wondering whether she should leave him. “Maybe,” she thinks, “it was better to make important decisions in the misty neverland of a tired mind, when she might be closer to her real desires, than using all the well-rested but suspect might of logic.” It’s one of many moments in Jeff Bartsch’s new novel, “Two Across,” where we’re reminded that brainpower can be vastly overrated, especially in matters of the heart.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/16/books/review/0816-BKS-Rosenstrach/0816-BKS-Rosenstrach-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 645, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/08/16/books/review/0816-BKS-Rosenstrach/0816-BKS-Rosenstrach-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 645}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/16/books/review/0816-BKS-Rosenstrach/0816-BKS-Rosenstrach-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 725, 'width': 674, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/16/books/review/0816-BKS-Rosenstrach/0816-BKS-Rosenstrach-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 725, 'width': 674, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/16/books/review/0816-BKS-Rosenstrach/0816-BKS-Rosenstrach-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/08/16/books/review/0816-BKS-Rosenstrach/0816-BKS-Rosenstrach-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/16/books/review/0816-BKS-Rosenstrach/0816-BKS-Rosenstrach-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Jeff Bartsch’s ‘Two Across’', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Without a Clue', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bartsch, Jeffrey C (1967- )', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Two Across (Book)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-08-14T14:55:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jenny Rosenstrach', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jenny', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rosenstrach', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/f71adc8d-618e-5724-9f3d-e9e8919cd57f', 'word_count': 584, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f71adc8d-618e-5724-9f3d-e9e8919cd57f'}, {'abstract': 'Louis de Bernières’s historical novel follows a wealthy British family through the tumultuous first decades of the 20th century.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/08/16/books/review/the-dust-that-falls-from-dreams-by-louis-de-bernieres.html', 'snippet': 'Louis de Bernières’s historical novel follows a wealthy British family through the tumultuous first decades of the 20th century.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Enviously noting the insatiable public appetite for television dramas like “Downton Abbey,” writers often lament the prospects for their historical novels, which have faint hopes of attracting anything resembling such sizable audiences. Not Louis de Bernières, who charges straight at them with “The Dust That Falls From Dreams,” a sprawling story about the members of a wealthy English family and the neighbors, friends and servants who constellate around them at their grand house in London during the first few decades of the 20th century. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘The Dust That Falls From Dreams,’ by Louis de Bernières', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Keep the Home Fires Burning', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Boyagoda, Randy', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'de Bernieres, Louis', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Dust That Falls From Dreams (Book)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-08-14T14:57:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Randy Boyagoda', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Randy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Boyagoda', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/3df21061-8b66-56d8-9113-5fbdad6e2693', 'word_count': 627, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3df21061-8b66-56d8-9113-5fbdad6e2693'}, {'abstract': 'A novelist imagines an incident that inspired George Eliot’s “Daniel Deronda.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/08/16/books/review/sophie-and-the-sibyl-by-patricia-duncker.html', 'snippet': 'A novelist imagines an incident that inspired George Eliot’s “Daniel Deronda.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'In 1863, George Eliot received the largest advance that had yet been offered for a novel; the £7,000 paid for “Romola” were well-earned. Her previous books had captured a zeitgeist. They received rave reviews, sold enviably well and were even quoted in debates in Parliament. Her stories were much talked about, but Eliot’s authorship and life were also subjects of gossip and speculation; writing under a pen name, the journalist and translator Marian Evans was also scandalously cohabiting with a married man. Evans was a social pariah, but Eliot was now a success.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/16/books/review/16baker-sub/16baker-sub-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 746, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/08/16/books/review/16baker-sub/16baker-sub-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 746}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/16/books/review/16baker-sub/16baker-sub-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 901, 'width': 725, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/16/books/review/16baker-sub/16baker-sub-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 901, 'width': 725, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/16/books/review/16baker-sub/16baker-sub-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/08/16/books/review/16baker-sub/16baker-sub-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/16/books/review/16baker-sub/16baker-sub-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘Sophie and the Sibyl,’ by Patricia Duncker', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Gambler', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Duncker, Patricia', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Sophie and the Sibyl: A Victorian Romance (Book)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-08-14T14:58:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jo Baker', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jo', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Baker', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/ffe39dd1-a555-574c-8f30-6a489951fc1b', 'word_count': 604, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ffe39dd1-a555-574c-8f30-6a489951fc1b'}, {'abstract': 'A native daughter’s return to a town of oddballs leads to fashion makeovers, and revenge.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/08/16/books/review/the-dressmaker-by-rosalie-ham.html', 'snippet': 'A native daughter’s return to a town of oddballs leads to fashion makeovers, and revenge.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Rosalie Ham’s novel “The Dressmaker” was published in Australia in 2000 and later this year will be presented as a movie starring Kate Winslet. What’s surprising is that a film version took so long to appear. From the moment the book opens, with a long shot of “wheat-yellow plains” around the “dark blot” of a hill in the small country town of Dungatar, we sense that we’re on our way to quirky-indie-movie land; and once the camera closes in on the “tumbling brown house” of Mad \xadMolly, “leaning provocatively on the grassy curve,” it’s clear we’re visiting a small 1950s town not of history but as imagined by Tim Burton: the gothic, polarized world of “Edward Scissorhands.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/16/books/review/16CLANCHY/16CLANCHY-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 750, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/08/16/books/review/16CLANCHY/16CLANCHY-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 750}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/16/books/review/16CLANCHY/16CLANCHY-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 820, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/16/books/review/16CLANCHY/16CLANCHY-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1981, 'width': 1585, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/16/books/review/16CLANCHY/16CLANCHY-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/08/16/books/review/16CLANCHY/16CLANCHY-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/16/books/review/16CLANCHY/16CLANCHY-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘The Dressmaker,’ by Rosalie Ham', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Done Up, Then Undone', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ham, Rosalie (1955- )', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Dressmaker (Book)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-08-14T14:59:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kate Clanchy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kate', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Clanchy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/00e561cf-912c-5222-8c8a-54aa2ddf2d2d', 'word_count': 604, 'uri': 'nyt://article/00e561cf-912c-5222-8c8a-54aa2ddf2d2d'}, {'abstract': '“Voices in the Ocean,” Susan Casey’s look at dolphin intelligence, is new at No. 13 on the hardcover nonfiction list.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/08/23/books/review/inside-the-list.html', 'snippet': '“Voices in the Ocean,” Susan Casey’s look at dolphin intelligence, is new at No. 13 on the hardcover nonfiction list.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Dolphin Whisperer: One of the more entertaining figures in “Voices in the Ocean,” Susan Casey’s look at dolphin intelligence — new at No. 13 on the hardcover nonfiction list — is the neurophysiologist John C. Lilly, who before his death in 2001 did as much as anyone to promote the image of dolphins as a nearly alien superspecies that might speak English someday. In the 1960s, Lilly’s unfettered enthusiasm caught on big with a certain segment of the public, but his fellow scientists were less enthralled: As Casey notes, the trade reviews of his 1961 book, “Man and Dolphin,” were scathing. “Scientifically unsound and naïve,” Natural History fumed, judging the book a perfect example of “how not to do scientific research.” The biologist E.O. Wilson called it “borderline irresponsible,” and a Navy researcher snarked: “I find Dr. Lilly’s work very interesting. I like Dr. Seuss, too.” Not that the reviews hurt the book’s prospects: “Man and Dolphin” hit the New York Times best-seller list at No.\u202f15. But Lilly’s reputation as a scientist never quite recovered. “Lilly is so fascinating because he’s so polarizing,” Casey told “Fresh Air” this month. “He was pretty freewheeling about the things he did and the things he said.” Or, as she clarifies in “Voices in the Ocean,” \u2009“The real damage occurred when Lilly began a run of experiments that involved LSD and interspecies sex.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '34', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/13/books/review/13casey/13casey-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 452, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/13/books/review/13casey/13casey-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 452, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/13/books/review/13casey/13casey-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/08/13/books/review/13casey/13casey-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/13/books/review/13casey/13casey-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Inside the List', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Inside the List', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-08-14T15:02:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gregory Cowles', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gregory', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cowles', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/117ef13a-1922-5c65-9951-734aa17d24e8', 'word_count': 493, 'uri': 'nyt://article/117ef13a-1922-5c65-9951-734aa17d24e8'}, {'abstract': 'Jessica Lahey literally wrote the book about letting kids fail. But sometimes her kids want her to step in, not back.', 'web_url': 'https://parenting.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/08/14/mom-i-know-youre-doing-that-gift-of-failure-thing-but-could-you-please-just-not/', 'snippet': 'Jessica Lahey literally wrote the book about letting kids fail. But sometimes her kids want her to step in, not back.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In an earlier post, Six Things Parents Need to Send Kids Back to School (That We Can’t Buy), I mentioned that I needed time to read, because books like “The Gift of Failure” by Jessica Lahey are inspiring me to push my children, not toward conventional school and other successes, but toward the kind of independence that leads them to find their own way. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/13/us/motherlode-failure/motherlode-failure-jumbo.png', 'height': 652, 'width': 500, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/13/us/motherlode-failure/motherlode-failure-superJumbo.png', 'height': 652, 'width': 500, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/13/us/motherlode-failure/motherlode-failure-thumbStandard.png', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/08/13/us/motherlode-failure/motherlode-failure-thumbStandard.png', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/13/us/motherlode-failure/motherlode-failure-thumbLarge.png', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Mom, I Know You’re Doing that ‘Gift of Failure’ Thing, But Could You Please Just Not?', 'kicker': 'Motherlode', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2015-08-14T15:02:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Styles', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': "By Kj Dell'Antonia", 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kj', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': "Dell'Antonia", 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/be972741-0cf9-5cd3-9ee3-a0f711e58506', 'word_count': 1609, 'uri': 'nyt://article/be972741-0cf9-5cd3-9ee3-a0f711e58506'}, {'abstract': 'Thirty years’ worth of stories, often about eccentric people, from a British master.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/08/16/books/review/in-another-country-by-david-constantine.html', 'snippet': 'Thirty years’ worth of stories, often about eccentric people, from a British master.', 'lead_paragraph': 'More than a century ago, in England during the late-Victorian and Edwardian eras, the god Pan made something of a comeback. In “The Wind in the Willows,” the Mole and the Water Rat encountered him piping at the gates of dawn. E.M. Forster located him in the Italian countryside in “The Story of a Panic,” and presiding over a secluded English pond in “A Room With a View.” Arthur Machen’s brilliant novella “The Great God Pan” emphasized the orgiastic horror as well as the freedom associated with the deity. And Peter Pan himself, that eminent Edwardian, recalled the god both in his name and in his occasional habit of blowing on the panpipes.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/16/books/review/16ALLEN/0816-BKS-Allen-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 480, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/08/16/books/review/16ALLEN/0816-BKS-Allen-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 480}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/16/books/review/16ALLEN/0816-BKS-Allen-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 819, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/16/books/review/16ALLEN/0816-BKS-Allen-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1127, 'width': 1409, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/16/books/review/16ALLEN/0816-BKS-Allen-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/08/16/books/review/16ALLEN/0816-BKS-Allen-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/16/books/review/16ALLEN/0816-BKS-Allen-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘In Another Country,’ by David Constantine', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'On the Edge', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Constantine, David (1944- )', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'In Another Country: Selected Stories (Book)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-08-14T15:03:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brooke Allen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brooke', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Allen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/3f7021f5-fe95-5f45-ad77-8003a6c26f21', 'word_count': 910, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3f7021f5-fe95-5f45-ad77-8003a6c26f21'}, {'abstract': 'Leave memories of the school cafeteria behind: With the addition of lobster, succotash becomes a deluxe main dish.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/08/19/dining/yes-succotash-has-a-luxurious-side.html', 'snippet': 'Leave memories of the school cafeteria behind: With the addition of lobster, succotash becomes a deluxe main dish.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Succotash, that savory mélange of corn and beans, is a noble dish with a long history. We have 17th-century Native Americans to thank for it; they introduced the stew to the struggling colonial immigrants. Composed of ingredients unknown in Europe at the time, it gradually became a standard meal in the settlers’ kitchens.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'u