e', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kevin McGarry', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kevin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'McGarry', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c0a301e3-bf9d-529b-af64-256764eb5e8a', 'word_count': 337, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c0a301e3-bf9d-529b-af64-256764eb5e8a'}, {'abstract': 'The president has been making more use of his power to designate national monuments. Here are two more he should consider.', 'web_url': 'https://takingnote.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/08/14/president-obamas-eleventh-hour-conservation-efforts/', 'snippet': 'The president has been making more use of his power to designate national monuments. Here are two more he should consider.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Shortly before leaving town for the summer, Congress approved – and President Obama happily signed into law – three bills that, taken together, will preserve as permanent wilderness roughly 275,000 acres of spectacular mountain terrain in Idaho known as\xa0 Boulder-White Clouds.\xa0 This was a rare moment for a Congress that has been far more interested in \xa0party infighting than environmental stewardship, and a tribute to the perseverance of one person, Congressman Mike Simpson, Republican of Idaho, who spent years engaging with local officials, ranchers, \xa0hunters, tribes, off-road vehicle enthusiasts and other stakeholders.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/14/opinion/14sempleWEB/14sempleWEB-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 451, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/08/14/opinion/14sempleWEB/14sempleWEB-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 451}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/14/opinion/14s