arge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/17/opinion/17heng/17heng-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'On China’s Economy', 'kicker': 'Heng', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Economic Conditions and Trends', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-08-16T21:22:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/ab815a4e-06ba-5053-8c96-3e303bdcd216', 'word_count': 5, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ab815a4e-06ba-5053-8c96-3e303bdcd216'}, {'abstract': 'A collection of the label’s artists, featured on the compilation “Wondaland Presents: The Eephus,” made a statement of aesthetic protest as well a political protest.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/08/17/arts/music/janelle-monae-and-other-wondaland-artists-step-back-in-time-at-the-highline-ballroom.html', 'snippet': 'A collection of the label’s artists, featured on the compilation “Wondaland Presents: The Eephus,” made a statement of aesthetic protest as well a political protest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Janelle Monáe is on her third, maybe fourth life, a striking fact given that she’s been an avowed futurist from the very start. For almost a decade, she’s been treating R&B and funk as technologies badly in need of updating, planning for their cyborg future and waiting for everyone else to catch up.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/17/arts/17MONAE/17MONAE-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xl