': [{'firstname': 'Jon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Caramanica', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/798f5d9b-67f3-5def-ad87-fa578a9a5152', 'word_count': 1047, 'uri': 'nyt://article/798f5d9b-67f3-5def-ad87-fa578a9a5152'}, {'abstract': '“A Manual for Cleaning Women” collects 43 stories by Ms. Berlin, who died a little over a decade ago and whose darkly wry work has remained unknown to many.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/08/17/books/lucia-berlins-roving-rowdy-life-is-reflected-in-a-book-of-her-stories.html', 'snippet': '“A Manual for Cleaning Women” collects 43 stories by Ms. Berlin, who died a little over a decade ago and whose darkly wry work has remained unknown to many.', 'lead_paragraph': '“In order to write about life, first you must live it.” That’s often attributed to Hemingway, and he would have applauded Lucia Berlin, who made fiction from her life and had a surplus of raw material. Born in Alaska in 1936 to a father who was a mining engineer, Ms. Berlin lived all over the American West and in Chile, New York City and Mexico. She survived childhood abuse and adult alcoholism, an addiction that sent her, in her words, to “jails, hospitals, psych wards.” She hung out with poets and musicians (marrying two jazz players). By her early 30s, she had been divorced three times and had four sons. She worked as a house cleaner, a substitute teacher and a hospital clerk. She put much of her roving, rowdy life onto the page in vivid stories that garnered the respect of a modest audience and now could be on the verge of making her posthumously famous.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/17/arts/17BOOKJP-SUB/17BOOKJP-SUB-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 428, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/08/17/arts/17BOOKJP-SUB/17BOOKJP-SUB-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 428}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/17/arts/17BOOKJP-SUB/17BOOKJP-SUB-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 731, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/17/arts/17BOOKJP-SUB/17BOOKJP-SUB-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1199, 'width': 1680, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/17/arts/17BOOKJP-SUB/17BOOKJP-SUB-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/08/17/arts/17BOOKJP-SUB/17BOOKJP-SUB-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/17/arts/17BOOKJP-SUB/17BOOKJP-SUB-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Lucia Berlin’s Roving, Rowdy Life Is Reflected in a Book of Her Stories', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Darkly Wry Stories Reflecting a Roving, Rowdy Life', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Berlin, Lucia (1936-2004)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'A Manual for Cleaning Women (Book)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-08-16T21:39:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Books', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Williams', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Williams', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5ad593ee-3546-551c-8b67-e4b6cc4e9bad', 'word_count': 1098, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5ad593ee-3546-551c-8b67-e4b6cc4e9bad'}, {'abstract': 'Ms. Barker’s betrayal-filled third novel is a 1,500-year gallop through the history of an unruly nation whose only constant, it seems, has been an inability to come to grips with its past.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/08/17/books/review-in-susan-barkers-the-incarnations-lives-of-despair-past-and-present-in-china.html', 'snippet': 'Ms. Barker’s betrayal-filled third novel is a 1,500-year gallop through the history of an unruly nation whose only constant, it seems, has been an inability to come to grips with its past.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Trying to spruce up its public image before the 2008 Summer Olympics, China embarked on a program of enforced self-improvement in which citizens were meant to change their ways on the spot. No more spitting; no more queue-jumping. No more risible translation of signs, either: In one fell swoop, the Dongda Anus Hospital, a much-photographed Beijing tourist attraction, became the Dongda Proctology Hospital. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/17/arts/17BOOKJP/17BOOKJP-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 900, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/08/17/arts/17BOOKJP/17BOOKJP-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 900}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/17/arts/17BOOKJP/17BO