5862-b570-a525781e9b65'}, {'abstract': 'For a project in a new hotel, Jonathan Waxman is featuring holdovers from the 1980s menu from the restaurant on the Upper East Side.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/08/19/dining/a-jams-revival-in-spirit.html', 'snippet': 'For a project in a new hotel, Jonathan Waxman is featuring holdovers from the 1980s menu from the restaurant on the Upper East Side.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Jams The California-style restaurant Jams, a trailblazer in New York when it opened on East 79th Street in 1984, remained part of Jonathan Waxman’s persona long after it closed. Now, for a restaurant project in a new hotel, Mr. Waxman is reviving Jams in spirit, though not in detail. It is an airy space done in stone and wood, and more rustic than the original, with simpler table settings and dishtowel napkins. Some of the 100 seats are at a long bar, and an open kitchen anchors the rear of the space. Along with grilled chicken with tarragon butter; and red pepper latkes with smoked salmon, corn and California caviar — which are holdovers from the 1980s — Jams will serve brightly accented dishes like rigatoni with crab meat and chile as well as swordfish with grilled pineapple salsa. “It’s my food, less Italian than Barbuto, but still what I like to cook,” Mr. Waxman said. Barbuto is his West Village restaurant, and its pastry chef Heather Miller is making the desserts at Jams, too. Melvyn Master, the wine expert who was Mr. Waxman’s partner and the “m’ in the name (Jams stands for Jonathan and Melvyn) is providing some of his wines to the new restaurant: 1 Hotel Central Park, 1414 Avenue of the Americas (58th Street), 212-703-2007.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/19/dining/19OFF/19OFF-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 417, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'leg