ille has a variety of home options, from spacious Victorians to smaller ranches and updated bungalows.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/08/23/realestate/amityville-ny-laced-with-canals.html', 'snippet': 'Amityville has a variety of home options, from spacious Victorians to smaller ranches and updated bungalows.', 'lead_paragraph': 'After Sage and Eric Meyer were married in 2009, they stepped up their saving for a house. They were renting in Amity Harbor, N.Y., bordering the village of Amityville; avid swimmers and boaters,\xa0they knew they wanted to buy on the water. The couple also wanted to live near Amityville Memorial High School, where Ms. Meyer was an English teacher.', 'print_section': 'RE', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/23/realestate/23LIVING-AMITYVILLE-slide-1FYC-copy/23LIVING-AMITYVILLE-slide-1FYC-copy-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/08/23/realestate/23LIVING-AMITYVILLE-slide-1FYC-copy/23LIVING-AMITYVILLE-slide-1FYC-copy-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/23/realestate/23LIVING-AMITYVILLE-slide-1FYC-copy/23LIVING-AMITYVILLE-slide-1FYC-copy-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/23/realestate/23LIVING-AMITYVILLE-slide-1FYC-copy/23LIVING-AMITYVILLE-slide-1FYC-copy-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1364, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/23/realestate/23LIVING-AMITYVILLE-slide-1FYC-copy/23LIVING-AMITYVILLE-slide-1FYC-copy-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/08/23/realestate/23LIVING-AMITYVILLE-slide-1FYC-copy/23LIVING-AMITYVILLE-slide-1FYC-copy-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/23/realestate/23LIVING-AMITYVILLE-slide-1FYC-copy/23LIVING-AMITYVILLE-slide-1FYC-copy-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Amityville, N.Y., Laced With Canals', 'kicker': 'Living In', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Quaint, and Laced With Canals ', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Real Estate and Housing (Residential)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'AMITYVILLE (NY)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-08-19T08:57:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'RealEstate', 'section_name': 'Real Estate', 'byline': {'original': 'By Karin Lipson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Karin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lipson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/798f3d48-ee0b-5ce2-a800-855f9d755d51', 'word_count': 1250, 'uri': 'nyt://article/798f3d48-ee0b-5ce2-a800-855f9d755d51'}, {'abstract': 'A seventh grader who survived a near-fatal accident faces evolving friendships and new pitfalls in Rebecca Stead’s novel.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/08/23/books/review/rebecca-steads-goodbye-stranger.html', 'snippet': 'A seventh grader who survived a near-fatal accident faces evolving friendships and new pitfalls in Rebecca Stead’s novel.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It’s really no wonder 12-year-olds appear frequently not only in novels written for children but also in those written for adults. Taken together, the characters of some classic literature might give the appearance of a middle-school class trip — with the likes of Carson McCullers, William Golding and Vladimir Nabokov in charge, making use of the power of being 12, which is essentially the power of the threshold. If you write a book about characters of this age, you get to document that fleeting moment of change from childhood to not-childhood, when the only person looking out for you might well be you. McCullers’s brilliant 1946 novel “The Member of the Wedding” used a spiky, soulful 12-year-old girl, Frankie Addams, as a vessel for ideas about, among other things, aloneness — both the day-to-day kind and the mortality kind. Frankie’s yearning to be part of something bigger consumes her. As she describes her brother and his bride, “They are the we of me.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/23/books/review/23stead/23stead-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 768, 'width': 512, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/23/books/review/23stead/23stead-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 768, 'width': 512, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/23/books/review/23stead/23stead-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/08/23/books/review/23stead/23stead-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/23/books/review/23stead/23stead-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Rebecca Stead’s ‘Goodbye Stranger’', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Selves and Selfies', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wolitzer, Meg', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Stead, Rebecca', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Goodbye Stranger (Book)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-08-19T09:00:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Meg Wolitzer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Meg', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wolitzer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/4289e3db-8b26-5830-a34c-81b0aa681b63', 'word_count': 908, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4289e3db-8b26-5830-a34c-81b0aa681b63'}, {'abstract': 'A bibliophile’s new full-time job disrupts her cherished work-life equilibrium.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/08/23/books/review/a-window-opens-by-elisabeth-egan.html', 'snippet': 'A bibliophile’s new full-time job disrupts her cherished work-life equilibrium.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Early on in Elisabeth Egan’s debut novel, “A Window Opens,” the narrator and main character, Alice Pearse — part-time magazine editor, full-time wife and mother of three children under the age of 12 — self-consciously invokes that familiar cultural catchphrase, “work-life balance,” admitting she’s “the tiniest bit smug” about her own equilibrium. “I wasn’t part of the high-powered commuter set and I wasn’t a PTA insider,” Alice says, “but I got to dip into both worlds.” Think of it as the Chekhov’s gun of chick-lit: If, in \xadChapter\u202f2, a character appreciates her tidily arranged life, you can bet she’s on the verge of losing her sanctimonious footing. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/23/books/review/23FORTINI/23FORTINI-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 814, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/08/23/books/review/23FORTINI/23FORTINI-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 814}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/23/books/review/23FORTINI/23FORTINI-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 755, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/23/books/review/23FORTINI/23FORTINI-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1510, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/23/books/review/23FORTINI/23FORTINI-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/08/23/books/review/23FORTINI/23FORTINI-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/23/books/review/23FORTINI/23FORTINI-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘A Window Opens,’ by Elisabeth Egan', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Overbooked', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Fortini, Amanda', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Egan, Elisabeth (1973- )', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'A Window Opens (Book)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-08-19T09:00:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Amanda Fortini', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Amanda', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fortini', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/441b94d3-f93e-5b9d-a6f9-8ba557743dfd', 'word_count': 1096, 'uri': 'nyt://article/441b94d3-f93e-5b9d-a6f9-8ba557743dfd'}, {'abstract': 'What happened when two politicians and a tech billionaire set out to reform a city’s schools.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/08/23/books/review/the-prize-by-dale-russakoff.html', 'snippet': 'What happened when two politicians and a tech billionaire set out to reform a city’s schools.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In America, education was long seen as the great equalizer, but that has become mostly myth. So, over the past decade, there has been a vigorous effort to fortify and rebuild our schools, and in this there is a recognition that we have failed our children, especially those living in poverty, those for whom education could — and should — be transformational. From Chicago to New Orleans, school reform has been engineered by the well heeled and well connected — from hedge fund managers to corporate heads to directors of foundations — who believe that with the right kind of teachers and pedagogy, and with a \xadbusiness-like administration, schooling can trump the daily burdens and indignities of growing up poor. “No excuses” has become the rallying cry of the reformers.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/23/books/review/23Kotlowitz/23Kotlowitz-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 461, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/08/23/books/review/23Kotlowitz/23Kotlowitz-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 461}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/23/books/review/23Kotlowitz/23Kotlowitz-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 786, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/23/books/review/23Kotlowitz/23Kotlowitz-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1572, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/23/books/review/23Kotlowitz/23Kotlowitz-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/08/23/books/review/23Kotlowitz/23Kotlowitz-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/23/books/review/23Kotlowitz/23Kotlowitz-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘The Prize,’ by Dale Russakoff', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Getting Schooled', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kotlowitz, Alex', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name':