rstname': 'Nate', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Chinen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b36b7349-bf06-520c-b069-b02e6dc455fc', 'word_count': 177, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b36b7349-bf06-520c-b069-b02e6dc455fc'}, {'abstract': '“Pearl” is an ambitious program that blends East and West in telling the story of this Nobel- and Pulitzer Prize-winning writer.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/08/23/arts/dance/a-dance-theater-work-explores-the-life-of-pearl-s-buck.html', 'snippet': '“Pearl” is an ambitious program that blends East and West in telling the story of this Nobel- and Pulitzer Prize-winning writer.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Come fall, you can expect an avalanche of biopics jockeying for awards consideration; Hollywood loves dramatic life stories. Dance, on the other hand, has never been particularly interested in biographical narrative. So “Pearl,” an ambitious dance-theater work exploring the life of the Nobel- and Pulitzer Prize-winning writer Pearl S. Buck, is a rare breed in both form and content. Buck was born in West Virginia and spent decades in China. She was a prolific writer (best known for the 1931 novel “The Good Earth”), an adoption advocate and a feminist — an intriguing muse for a dance spectacle.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/23/arts/23GUIDE4/23GUIDE4-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 257, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/08/23/arts/23GUIDE4/23GUIDE4-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 257}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/23/arts/23GUIDE4/23GUIDE4-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 438, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'ju