Boulez’s works for piano solo. On Aug. 24, Mr. Kigawa returns to the same space with pieces in the Boulez orbit, by one of his great precursors and by two who’ve been influenced by him. The concert includes both books of Debussy’s “Images” alongside Marco Stroppa’s “Traiettoria ... deviata” for piano and live electronics and Tristan Murail’s “Territoires de l’oubli.” (7:30 p.m., lepoissonrouge.com.)', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/23/arts/23GUIDE5/23GUIDE5-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 401, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/08/23/arts/23GUIDE5/23GUIDE5-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 401}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/23/arts/23GUIDE5/23GUIDE5-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 684, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/23/arts/23GUIDE5/23GUIDE5-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1367, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/23/arts/23GUIDE5/23GUIDE5-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/08/23/arts/23GUIDE5/23GUIDE5-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/23/arts/23GUIDE5/23GUIDE5-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Pianist Returns to Le Poisson Rouge, and LoftOpera Heads to Brooklyn', 'kicker': 'Classical', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Solo Piano, Multiple Voices', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kigawa, Taka', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-08-19T15:05:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Zachary Woolfe', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Zachary', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Woolfe', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b6acec10-f43d-58cb-a4f3-9f18b63a19d6', 'word_count': 144, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b6acec10-f43d-58cb-a4f3-9f18b63a19d6'}, {'abstract': 'The IFC Center will host a retrospective of his films, including “Wings of Desire,” “Paris, Texas” and “Alice in the Cities.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/08/23/movies/the-imperfect-wim-wenders-touring-films-by-the-german-director.html', 'snippet': 'The IFC Center will host a retrospective of his films, including “Wings of Desire,” “Paris, Texas” and “Alice in the Cities.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'The German director Wim Wenders has a pile of awards from Cannes and other festivals as well as three Oscar nominations, but American moviegoers who stick to blockbusters probably remain unfamiliar with him. Perhaps a touring retrospective organized by Janus Films that begins a monthlong run on Aug. 28 at the IFC Center will serve as a small corrective.\xa0It features 12 of his films, including early works and some of his better-known movies from the 1980s. Among those is the haunting “Wings of Desire,” a fantasy about angels who eavesdrop on and sometimes comfort inhabitants of Berlin.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': 