ment_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Hiroko Tabuchi', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Hiroko', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tabuchi', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e6e1344c-d509-5040-b4f8-669a15ab3bfe', 'word_count': 680, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e6e1344c-d509-5040-b4f8-669a15ab3bfe'}, {'abstract': 'The Houston rapper Pimp C, who died in 2007, is now the subject of a biography — and a vivid memory for a New York Times journalist.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/08/23/arts/music/pimp-c-in-a-journalists-back-pages.html', 'snippet': 'The Houston rapper Pimp C, who died in 2007, is now the subject of a biography — and a vivid memory for a New York Times journalist.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I can tell you about the moment I heard Pimp C was dead — Dec. 4, 2007; at work in my office at Vibe; nervously refreshing TMZ, which had broken the news; feeling nauseous. The room hummed with the white noise that takes over when you no longer register what’s happening around you. Minutes later, a colleague knocked on my door, disrupting the quiet, and we looked at each other wordlessly, our jaws quivering but holding firm. My boss came by to ask if I was all right. I wasn’t.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/23/arts/23HOUSTON-RAP/23HOUSTON-RAP-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 382, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/08/23/arts/23HOUSTON-RAP/23HOUSTON-RAP-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 382}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/23/arts/23HOUSTON-RAP/23HOUSTON-RAP-jumbo-v