Dominic P. Papatola', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dominic', 'middlename': 'P.', 'lastname': 'Papatola', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ab27c0c5-7112-5d28-a79f-e8c23847355e', 'word_count': 1473, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ab27c0c5-7112-5d28-a79f-e8c23847355e'}, {'abstract': 'This spinoff of “The Walking Dead,” which is beginning a six-episode run on AMC, opens with society still in denial about its lurching adversaries.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/08/21/arts/television/review-fear-the-walking-dead-a-show-about-a-plagues-start-what-zombies.html', 'snippet': 'This spinoff of “The Walking Dead,” which is beginning a six-episode run on AMC, opens with society still in denial about its lurching adversaries.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It’s either surprising or completely logical that “The Walking Dead” has become the most popular drama on television (by some measures) while also being one of the most unusual. The all-bets-are-off gore and violence made possible by its post-zombie-apocalypse scenario set it apart, of course. But even more distinctive are its dawdling rhythms, laconic performances and willingness to turn down the sound. Few shows, if any, are as quiet as “The Walking Dead.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/20/arts/21DEAD/21DEAD-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/08/20/arts/21DEAD/21DEAD-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/20/arts/21DEAD/21DEAD-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'ran