'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/5303fedd-c5d7-5877-9f10-ca396e7188b3', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/5303fedd-c5d7-5877-9f10-ca396e7188b3'}, {'abstract': 'Worpswede has played host to creatives from Rainer Maria Rilke to Josephine Meckseper — and is now home to an ambitious residency program.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/08/20/t-magazine/worpswede-artist-colony-germany.html', 'snippet': 'Worpswede has played host to creatives from Rainer Maria Rilke to Josephine Meckseper — and is now home to an ambitious residency program.', 'lead_paragraph': '“How large the eyes become here! They want at all times to possess the whole sky,” wrote the young poet Rainer Maria Rilke in 1900. He had just arrived at Worpswede, the fabled artist colony on a stretch of German moorland so flat it becomes almost round as the view curves along the earth’s surface with scarcely a foothill, mountain or other perpendicular obstruction in sight.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/20/t-magazine/20tmag-worpswede1/20tmag-worpswede1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/08/20/t-magazine/20tmag-worpswede1/20tmag-worpswede1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/20/t-magazine/20tmag-worpswede1/20tmag-worpswede1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/20/t-magazine/20tmag-worpswede1/20tmag-worpswede1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/20/t-magazine/20tmag-worpswede1/20tmag-worpswede1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/08/20/t-magazine/20tmag-worpswede1/20tmag-worpswede1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/20/t-magazine/20tmag-worpswede1/20tmag-worpswede1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A German Artist Colony, Suspended in Time, Stakes a Place in the Contemporary Scene', 'kicker': 'Travel', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Meckseper, Josephine', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rilke, Rainer Maria', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Germany', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-08-20T21:36:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'TStyle', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rachel Corbett', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rachel', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Corbett', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/345a7048-40d4-5a77-ae8a-f723a20679d3', 'word_count': 595, 'uri': 'nyt://article/345a7048-40d4-5a77-ae8a-f723a20679d3'}, {'abstract': 'Brian Kellow charts Ms. Mengers’s ruthless path to success with a client list that included Ryan and Tatum O’Neal, Barbra Streisand and Gene Hackman.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/08/21/books/review-can-i-go-now-a-biography-of-the-hollywood-agent-sue-mengers.html', 'snippet': 'Brian Kellow charts Ms. Mengers’s ruthless path to success with a client list that included Ryan and Tatum O’Neal, Barbra Streisand and Gene Hackman.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The biographer Brian Kellow, whose last subject was Pauline Kael, writes in “Can I Go Now?” that even he had doubts about whether the Hollywood agent Sue Mengers would make a good subject. The idea of doing a book about her came from his agent. And even allowing for any prejudices an agent might have about how interesting agents are, it was still a stretch. Ms. Mengers may have been the most glam, powerful, status-conscious, star-snuggling Hollywood power broker of the 1970s, but both she and her way of doing business had gone the way of the dinosaurs by the end of that decade. Now she is Sue Who? to the world at large.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/21/arts/21BOOK2/21BOOK2-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 913, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/08/21/arts/21BOOK2/21BOOK2-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 913}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/21/arts/21BOOK2/21BOOK2-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 673, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/21/arts/21BOOK2/21BOOK2-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1346, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/21/arts/21BOOK2/21BOOK2-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'c