640be404-f6b8-57a1-97d9-2f351f2fcabb'}, {'abstract': 'A 1946 Dial jazz recording session with Lucky Thompson on tenor saxophone strikes a mature ear differently than it did a young one.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/08/21/arts/music/vintage-sax-solo-improves-with-listeners-age.html', 'snippet': 'A 1946 Dial jazz recording session with Lucky Thompson on tenor saxophone strikes a mature ear differently than it did a young one.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Charlie Parker, the matchless alto saxophonist and bebop exemplar, had few better days in a recording studio than March 28, 1946. He was in Hollywood, leading his first session under an exclusive contract with Dial Records. His handpicked sidemen included the well-seasoned tenor saxophonist Lucky Thompson and a young trumpeter named Miles Davis, and by the end of the date they had recorded several of Parker’s most canonical tracks, including “A Night in Tunisia” and “Yardbird Suite.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/21/arts/21CHINEN1/21CHINEN1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 987, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/08/21/arts/21CHINEN1/21CHINEN1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 987}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/21/arts/21CHINEN1/21CHINEN1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 623, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/21/arts/21CHINEN1/21CHINEN1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1246, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/21/arts/21CHINEN1/21CHINEN1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/08/21/arts/21CHINEN1/21CHINEN1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/21/arts/21CHINEN1/21CHINEN1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Vintage Sax Solo Improves With (Listener’s) Age', 'kicker': 'The Art of Slow', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Sax Solo Improves With a Little Aging', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Jazz', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Parker, Charlie', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Thompson, Lucky', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-08-20T22:19:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nate Chinen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nate', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Chinen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e1d547e2-4a1e-5698-a196-a0913f33c5d8', 'word_count': 444, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e1d547e2-4a1e-5698-a196-a0913f33c5d8'}, {'abstract': 'As you grow older, Shakespeare’s characters and language tend to assert themselves in your consciousness in changing ways.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/08/21/theater/a-shakespeare-for-all-seasons.html', 'snippet': 'As you grow older, Shakespeare’s characters and language tend to assert themselves in your consciousness in changing ways.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For many theatergoers, summer is the season of Shakespeare, in the way it’s the season of corn and tomatoes for those who want these foods fresh from the garden. Like such comestibles, Shakespeare can be devoured in hearty single sittings — and often alfresco — what with a crop of productions that present the theatrical equivalent of a jolly picnic. For New Yorkers, an abundance of movable feasts is available, from city parks and parking lots to more sylvan environs upstate. (On my August schedule: yet another “Hamlet,” this one starring Benedict Cumberbatch, in London.)', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '24', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/21/arts/21BRANTLEY1/21BRANTLEY1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 841, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/08/21/arts/21BRANTLEY1/21BRANTLEY1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 841}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/21/arts/21BRANTLEY1/21BRANTLEY1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 731, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/21/arts/21BRANTLEY1/21BRANTLEY1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1462, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/21/arts/21BRANTLEY1/21BRANTLEY1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/08/21/arts/21BRANTLEY1/21BRANTLEY1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/21/arts/21BRANTLEY1/21BRANTLEY1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Shakespeare for All Seasons', 'kicker': 'The Art of Slow', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Seven Ages of Shakespeare', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Shakespeare, William', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-08-20T22:20:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Brantley', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Brantley', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/bcbc06c8-2c97-5dbf-b0b6-335ce03fe522', 'word_count': 505, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bcbc06c8-2c97-5dbf-b0b6-335ce03fe522'}, {'abstract': 'His films, like “Titicut Follies” and “At Berkeley,” examine places, institutions and social phenomena by slowly revealing the subtle layers and patterns in their topics.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/08/21/movies/frederick-wisemans-documentaries-defy-straightforward-plot.html', 'snippet': 'His films, like “Titicut Follies” and “At Berkeley,” examine places, institutions and social phenomena by slowly revealing the subtle layers and patterns in their topics.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The closer it hews to the conventions of journalism, the more documentary filmmaking becomes an art of compression and acceleration. You know the drill. A social problem is explored, a famous or obscure person’s life story is told, a historical episode is illuminated, in a familiar rhythm and pace. Talking-head interviews alternate with archival clips or re-enactments, all in the service of an argument or a plot. Usually it’s all over in about 90 minutes, and you take away a neat little package of outrage, empathy or enlightenment. Even long-form documentaries, like “The Jinx,” Andrew Jarecki’s mini-series on the alleged crimes of Robert Durst, follow this formula, in that they depend on the quickening of the viewer’s attention. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '24', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/21/arts/21SCOTT1/21SCOTT1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 399, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/08/21/arts/21SCOTT1/21SCOTT1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 399}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/21/arts/21SCOTT1/21SCOTT1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 681, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/21/arts/21SCOTT1/21SCOTT1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1363, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/21/arts/21SCOTT1/21SCOTT1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/08/21/arts/21SCOTT1/21SCOTT1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/21/arts/21SCOTT1/21SCOTT1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Frederick Wiseman’s Documentaries Defy Straightforward Plot', 'kicker': 'The Art of Slow', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Documentaries That Defy Plot ', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wiseman, Frederick', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Documentary Films and Programs', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-08-20T22:21:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By A.O. Scott', 'person': [{'firstname': 'A.', 'middlename': 'O.', 'lastname': 'Scott', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/050cf305-6b9a-58a3-93b9-a415790cf888', 'word_count': 549, 'uri': 'nyt://article/050cf305-6b9a-58a3-93b9-a415790cf888'}, {'abstract': 'The poet’s famous line — “I’m nobody! Who are you?” — is not what you want to hear when you are younger than 30.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/08/21/books/holding-off-emily-dickinsons-complete-poems.html', 'snippet': 'The poet’s famous line — “I’m nobody! Who are you?” — is not what you want to hear when you are younger than 30.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I’m embarrassed by how long I resisted Emily Dickinson’s “Complete Poems,” and I’m struck by how much my copy means to me now. As a daft young punk I too often sought out reckless emotion and vulgar effects, the same way one sometimes wants, when callow and feckless, to date a person with obvious physical attributes. Dickinson’s famous line — “I’m nobody! Who are you?” — is not what you want to hear when you are younger than 30, or, in my delayed maturity, closer to 40. It should have helped, but did not, when one of my favorite high school English teachers, Donald Glancy, explained that you could sing nearly all of Dickinson’s verse to the tune of “The Yellow Rose of Texas.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '24', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/21/arts/21GARNER1/21GARNER1-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 900, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/08/21/arts/21GARNER1/21GARNER1-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 900}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/21/arts/21GARNER1/21GARNER1-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 683, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/21/arts/21GARNER1/21GARNER1-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1365, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/21/arts/21GARNER1/21GARNER1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/08/21/arts/21GARNER1/21GARNER1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/21/arts/21GARNER1/21GARNER1-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Holding Off Emily Dickinson’s ‘Complete Poems’', 'kicker': 'The Art of Slow', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Dickinson, Forever Phosphorescent ', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Poetry and Poets', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dickinson, Emily', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-08-20T22:22:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Books', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dwight Garner', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dwight', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Garner', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/39f11db0-d371-5d7c-a190-203250b639fe', 'word_count': 392, 'uri': 'nyt://article/39f11db0-d371-5d7c-a190-203250b639fe'}, {'abstract': 'A work inspired by the life of the 11th-century Tibetan yogi and poet Jetsun Milarepa.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/08/21/arts/music/eliane-radigue-mining-wisdom-from-11th-century-buddhism.html', 'snippet': 'A work inspired by the life of the 11th-century Tibetan yogi and poet Jetsun Milarepa.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I came pretty late to “Jetsun Mila,” by the French composer Eliane Radigue, but it may be one of those things a lot of people will feel fine about coming to pretty late. Turn it on, and it just keeps teaching.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/21/arts/21RATLIFF1SUB2/21RATLIFF1SUB2-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 444, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/08/21/arts/21RATLIFF1SUB2/21RATLIFF1SUB2-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 444}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/21/arts/21RATLIFF1SUB2/21RATLIFF1SUB2-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 758, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/21/arts/21RATLIFF1SUB2/21RATLIFF1SUB2-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1516, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/21/arts/21RATLIFF1SUB2/21RATLIFF1SUB2-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/08/21/arts/21RATLIFF1SUB2/21RATLIFF1SUB2-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/21/arts/21RATLIFF1SUB2/21RATLIFF1SUB2-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Eliane Radigue, Mining Wisdom From 11th-Century Buddhism', 'kicker': 'The Art of Slow', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Aural Inspiration From a Sage Yogi ', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Radigue, Eliane', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-08-20T22:24:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Ratliff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ratliff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0530bceb-9b57-5954-944b-666357f8629e', 'word_count': 484, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0530bceb-9b57-5954-944b-666357f8629e'}, {'abstract': 'A dance critic learns to embrace the classicism and humanity of a ballet that at first seemed all surface.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/08/21/arts/dance/awakening-to-the-joys-of-the-sleeping-beauty.html', 'snippet': 'A dance critic learns to embrace the classicism and humanity of a ballet that at first seemed all surface.', 'lead_paragraph': 'At the age of 35, I tried counting. I found I’d seen the ballet “The Sleeping Beauty” 200 times. That was 1990, precisely a century after the composer Tchaikovsky and the choreographer Marius Petipa had first presented it in St. Petersburg. Since then, I’ve watched it on many more occasions — 10 so far in 2015 alone. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '24', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/21/arts/21MACCAULEY1/21MACCAULEY1-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 561, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/08/21/arts/21MACCAULEY1/21MACCAULEY1-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 561}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/21/arts/21MACCAULEY1/21MACCAULEY1-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 957, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/21/arts/21MACCAULEY1/21MACCAULEY1-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1914, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/21/arts/21MACCAULEY1/21MACCAULEY1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/08/21/arts/21MACCAULEY1/21MACCAULEY1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/21/arts/21MACCAULEY1/21MACCAULEY1-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Awakening to the Joys of ‘The Sleeping Beauty’', 'kicker': 'The Art of Slow', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Waking to the Joys of a Classic Ballet', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dancing', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Sleeping Beauty (Ballet)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Petipa, Marius', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tchaikovsky, Peter Ilyich', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-08-20T22:24:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Dance', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alastair Macaulay', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alastair', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Macaulay', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2e01fcb4-44f3-5ca8-9da6-b1c6210b460c', 'word_count': 573, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2e01fcb4-44f3-5ca8-9da6-b1c6210b460c'}, {'abstract': 'Giving up hours of a day to an immersive theater experience can be a particularly pleasurable escape from the world.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/08/21/theater/another-definition-for-extended-play.html', 'snippet': 'Giving up hours of a day to an immersive theater experience can be a particularly pleasurable escape from the world.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I like a short show. A 90-minute play means more time to write, more time to relax, a chance to see my husband before he goes to sleep, no tedious intermission. But I also have a sneaking fondness for longer plays. Much, much, much longer ones.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/21/arts/21SOLOSKI1SUB1/21SOLOSKI1SUB1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/08/21/arts/21SOLOSKI1SUB1/21SOLOSKI1SUB1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumb