6-c9ac-5312-98f3-da88b238038f', 'word_count': 493, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8a550a86-c9ac-5312-98f3-da88b238038f'}, {'abstract': 'Five works at MoMA summarize the artist’s quest to activate the surface of the canvas and open the viewer’s eyes.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/08/21/arts/design/mondrians-paintings-and-their-pulsating-intricacy.html', 'snippet': 'Five works at MoMA summarize the artist’s quest to activate the surface of the canvas and open the viewer’s eyes.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The longer I look at Mondrian’s paintings, the more I see in them. This applies to lots of art, but I think Mondrian built real time into his paintings. They unfold with unusual deliberation in a semblance of symmetry and order that is actually precarious, even volatile. This is especially true of his mature works from the late 1920s to the early 1940s, with their tensile fusions of glowing white backgrounds, black scaffoldings and blocks of bright primaries. Everything about them, the tiniest decision, is evident and has visual repercussions.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/21/arts/21SMITH1/21SMITH1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 380, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/08/21/arts/21SMITH1/21SMITH1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 380}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/21/arts/21SMITH1/21SMITH1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 648, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/21/arts/21SMITH1/21SMITH1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1295, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subt