matter, format and ability to balance mythology and procedural episodes.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Television is not an art of slow contemplation. Watching it in real time is more or less a requirement. (My DVR offers slow-motion playback, which has its uses, but they’re not artistic.)', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/21/arts/21HALE1/21HALE1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 897, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/08/21/arts/21HALE1/21HALE1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 897}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/21/arts/21HALE1/21HALE1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 685, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/21/arts/21HALE1/21HALE1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1370, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/21/arts/21HALE1/21HALE1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/08/21/arts/21HALE1/21HALE1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/21/arts/21HALE1/21HALE1-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Revisiting ‘The X-Files,’ a Series Worth Another Watch', 'kicker': 'The Art of Slow', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Paranormal Series Worth Rewatching ', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Carter, Chris C', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The X-Files (TV Program)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-08-20T22:27:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mike Hale', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mike', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hale', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2b407d3f-a7e2-5f55-b810-1ef302566eea', 'word_count': 359, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2b407d3f-a7e2-5f55-b810-1ef302566eea'}, {'abstract': 'A critic says he has come to appreciate this sitcom, even how it invents new ways for its core characters to behave badly.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/08/21/arts/television/its-always-sunny-in-philadelphia-converts-a-skeptic.html', 'snippet': 'A critic says he has come to appreciate this sitcom, even how it invents new ways for its core characters to behave badly.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For years I was put off by the unrelenting crudeness of the television comedy “It’s Always Sunny in Philadelphia.” In 2011 I wrote a minute-by-minute comparison of its Season 7 premiere with the Season 3 premiere of another crass show, “Blue Mountain State,” assessing which was more vulgar. It was a tongue-in-cheek article, but the tsk-tsking undertone was undeniable.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '25', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/21/arts/21GENZLINGER1SUB1/21GENZLINGER1SUB1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 407, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2