rson': [{'firstname': 'Neil', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Genzlinger', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a1b30f75-f78e-5ce1-9b82-2b544030a082', 'word_count': 356, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a1b30f75-f78e-5ce1-9b82-2b544030a082'}, {'abstract': 'It was deeper immersion in Wagner and Schubert that opened a music critic to Bruckner, the master Austrian symphonist.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/08/21/arts/music/for-bruckners-seventh-symphony-a-long-delayed-appreciation.html', 'snippet': 'It was deeper immersion in Wagner and Schubert that opened a music critic to Bruckner, the master Austrian symphonist.', 'lead_paragraph': 'By the time I was a music major in college, with all the composers of the past competing for my attention, I had decided I could take a pass on Anton Bruckner. Many great artists, including the conductor Bruno Walter, whom I idolized, championed Bruckner (1824-1896) as a master Austrian symphonist. But from my impatient samplings of those symphonies, Bruckner struck me as a kind of ponderously monumental Brahms, or an inert and stretched-out Mahler.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '25', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/21/arts/21TOMASSINI1/21TOMASSINI1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 740, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/08/21/arts/21TOMASSINI1/21TOMASSINI1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 740}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/21/arts/21TOMASSINI1/21TOMASSINI1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 831, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': '