html', 'snippet': 'An ambitious New Yorker insinuates herself into the old guard in the years before the financial crisis.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A quarter-century ago, Harper’s published an essay by Tom Wolfe titled “Stalking the Billion-Footed Beast: A \xadLiterary Manifesto for the New Social Novel.” Wolfe, whose best seller “The Bonfire of the Vanities” had been published two years earlier, called for a revival of the sort of novel that emerged during the 19th century: realist fiction that was wide in its scope and accurate in its detail, grounded in the kind of reporting that journalistic nonfiction demands.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/23/books/review/23MEADSUB/0823-BKS-MEAD-SUB-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 567, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/08/23/books/review/23MEADSUB/0823-BKS-MEAD-SUB-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 567}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/23/books/review/23MEADSUB/0823-BKS-MEAD-SUB-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 967, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/23/books/review/23MEADSUB/0823-BKS-MEAD-SUB-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1404, 'width': 1486, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/23/books/review/23MEADSUB/0823-BKS-MEAD-SUB-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/08/23/books/review/23MEADSUB/0823-BKS-MEAD-SUB-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/23/books/review/23MEADSUB/0823-BKS-MEAD-SUB-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘Everybody Rise,’ by Stephanie Clifford', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Social Network', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Clifford, Stephanie', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Everybody Rise (Book)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-08-21T14:37:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rebecca Mead', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rebecca', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mead', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/0dd43d37-0820-5a4d-a5eb-5d91622053ba', 'word_count': 1278, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0dd43d37-0820-5a4d-a5eb-5d91622053ba'}, {'abstract': 'A pair of scholars tease out the central notions that have given rise to the modern age. ', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/08/23/books/review/the-shape-of-the-new-by-scott-l-montgomery-and-daniel-chirot.html', 'snippet': 'A pair of scholars tease out the central notions that have given rise to the modern age. ', 'lead_paragraph': 'The world has been horrified but also puzzled by the rise of ISIS. How does one comprehend its brutality and success? What is its likely path? In March 2015, The Atlantic offered an answer, in an analysis by Graeme Wood that quickly became the most widely read essay in the magazine’s 158-year history. Titled “What ISIS Really Wants,” it focused on the ideology that animates the group. Understand its ideas, Wood suggested, and you will understand the phenomenon and how to fight it. Many other, more polemical explanations of jihadi terrorism today — from Bill Maher to Sam Harris — also shine a spotlight on the ideas behind the mayhem.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/23/books/review/0823-BKS-Zakaria/0823-BKS-Zakaria-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 740, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/08/23/books/review/0823-BKS-Zakaria/0823-BKS-Zakaria-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 740}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/23/books/review/0823-BKS-Zakaria/0823-BKS-Zakaria-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 830, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/23/books/review/0823-BKS-Zakaria/0823-BKS-Zakaria-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1660, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/23/books/review/0823-BKS-Zakaria/0823-BKS-Zakaria-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/08/23/books/review/0823-BKS-Zakaria/0823-BKS-Zakaria-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/23/books/review/0823-BKS-Zakaria/0823-BKS-Zakaria-thumbLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘The Shape of the New,’ by Scott L. Montgomery and Daniel Chirot', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Something in the Air', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Chirot, Daniel (1942- )', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Montgomery, Scott L (1951- )', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Shape of the New: Four Big Ideas and How They Made the Modern World (Book)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-08-21T14:38:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Fareed Zakaria', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Fareed', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Zakaria', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/168e3663-1e48-5ad0-be58-f50b4541d19f', 'word_count': 1615, 'uri': 'nyt://article/168e3663-1e48-5ad0-be58-f50b4541d19f'}, {'abstract': 'From his Midwest origins, the young T.S. Eliot moved on to the works that would make his name.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/08/23/books/review/young-eliot-by-robert-crawford.html', 'snippet': 'From his Midwest origins, the young T.S. Eliot moved on to the works that would make his name.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In an unheroic age, T.S. Eliot was \xadpoetry’s ideal antihero. Shy, indecisive, gangly, he essayed the Parnassian heights not baroquely turbaned like a Byron but buttoned down and gray-flanneled like a Walter Mitty. By day, he was a Lloyds banker in “a four-piece suit,” as Virginia Woolf reportedly described him; by night, a visionary poet, whose lyric fragments harmonized with the malaise troubling Europe after World War I. The Scottish poet Robert Crawford’s assiduous “Young Eliot” follows “Tom” from his earliest poetic finger exercises to the “ineradicably insinuating music” of “The Waste Land” (1922), in which April cruelly breeds lilacs as well as a bramble of textual notes.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/23/books/review/23YEZZI/23YEZZI-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 838, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/08/23/books/review/23YEZZI/23YEZZI-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 838}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/23/books/review/23YEZZI/23YEZZI-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 733, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/23/books/review/23YEZZI/23YEZZI-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1466, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/23/books/review/23YEZZI/23YEZZI-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/08/23/books/review/23YEZZI/23YEZZI-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/23/books/review/23YEZZI/23YEZZI-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘Young Eliot,’ by Robert Crawford', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Hallowed Man', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Crawford, Robert (1959- )', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Eliot, Thomas Stearns', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Poetry and Poets', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': "Young Eliot: From St. Louis to 'The Waste Land' (Book)", 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-08-21T14:38:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Yezzi', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Yezzi', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/277ed162-504a-5a10-b0b9-a4acf900c8c1', 'word_count': 1176, 'uri': 'nyt://article/277ed162-504a-5a10-b0b9-a4acf900c8c1'}, {'abstract': 'An inquiry into the history of the written word.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/08/23/books/review/palimpsest-a-history-of-the-written-word-by-matthew-battles.html', 'snippet': 'An inquiry into the history of the written word.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When an author assigns himself the task of compiling “a history of the written word,” a reader is likely to wonder: How well written is it? “Palimpsest” is a serious, well-researched book. Matthew Battles evokes the flexibility of human consciousness and the ability of the written word to absorb and deploy new technologies even though, as he explains, writing has always had its supposed shallows, from “penny dreadfuls” to propaganda and pornography. However, “Palimpsest” puts up a dull defense of its subject. It’s elliptically organized and full of workmanlike, hackneyed prose. It’s a history of the written word, but it’s also a repository of literary missteps.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘Palimpsest: A History of the Written Word,’ by Matthew Battles', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Scribal Arts', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Shields, David', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Battles, Matthew (1968- )', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Palimpsest: A History of the Written Word (Book)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-08-21T14:39:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Shields', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shields', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/568b6566-dde4-5a46-8190-e56222d502fe', 'word_count': 686, 'uri': 'nyt://article/568b6566-dde4-5a46-8190-e56222d502fe'}, {'abstract': 'Is it possible to study world religions in today’s climate?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/08/23/books/review/faith-ed-by-linda-k-wertheimer.html', 'snippet': 'Is it possible to study world religions in today’s climate?', 'lead_paragraph': 'What should you do in a house of worship of another faith? This is a question that vexes many who write about religion and many who do not. Should non-Jewish men don kipas in synagogues? Should non-Muslim women cover their heads in a mosque? Should non-Catholics kneel during Mass? When everyone at an evangelical church holds hands and prays, should I keep mine by my side? Does not participating imply rudeness or does it simply say, I respect your faith enough not to pretend I am a part of it?', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘Faith Ed.,’ by Linda K. Wertheimer', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'One Nation Under Gods', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Riley, Naomi Schaefer', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wertheimer, Linda K (1964- )', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Religion and Belief', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Faith Ed: Teaching About Religion in an Age of Intolerance (Book)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-08-21T14:39:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Naomi Schaefer Riley', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Naomi', 'middlename': 'Schaefer', 'lastname': 'Riley', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/72f40794-4a19-573a-ac2a-3560fb652f48', 'word_count': 810, 'uri': 'nyt://article/72f40794-4a19-573a-ac2a-3560fb652f48'}, {'abstract': 'The fantasy of Satanic abuse at preschools in the 1980s.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/08/23/books/review/we-believe-the-children-by-richard-beck.html', 'snippet': 'The fantasy of Satanic abuse at preschools in the 1980s.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the summer of 1983, a California woman named Judy Johnson took her 3-year-old son Matthew to a hospital near her home in Manhattan Beach. She pointed the doctor’s attention to her child’s anus, which, she said, looked redder than it had that morning, before he had gone to his day care center. Whether out of genuine concern about foul play or to salve a worried mother, the doctor filed a suspected child abuse report. Johnson, who later received a diagnosis of paranoid schizophrenia, took her own suspicions to the police, who soon alerted parents whose children attended the McMartin Preschool to a possible abuser in their midst.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/23/books/review/23HYMOWITZ/23Hymowitz-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 401, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/08/23/books/review/23HYMOWITZ/23Hymowitz-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 401}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/23/books/review/23HYMOWITZ/23Hymowitz-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 684, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/23/books/review/23HYMOWITZ/23Hymowitz-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1368, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/23/books/review/23HYMOWITZ/23HYMOWITZ-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/08/23/books/review/23HYMOWITZ/23HYMOWITZ-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/23/books/review/23HYMOWITZ/23HYMOWITZ-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘We Believe the Children,’ by Richard Beck', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Notes on a Scandal', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Beck, Richard (1986- )', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Child Abuse and Neglect', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'We Believe the Children: A Moral Panic in the 1980s (Book)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-08-21T14:41:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kay Hymowitz', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kay', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hymowitz', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/f2cb32e5-16a1-5177-af23-d789f421d187', 'word_count': 650, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f2cb32e5-16a1-5177-af23-d789f421d187'}, {'abstract': 'If you don’t have kids, you tend to identify with the children in any story.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/08/23/books/review/whose-side-are-you-on.html', 'snippet': 'If you don’t have kids, you tend to identify with the children in any story.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When I was 15, I was playing with my 2-year-old sister and our 19-month-old cousin by a fountain in Amherst, Mass. Someone smiled and said, “They’re yours?” and I was shocked. Even if I’d had them in middle school, it was mathematically impossible to have given birth to both of them. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '37', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/23/books/review/0823-BKS-Meloy/0823-BKS-Meloy-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 297, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/08/23/books/review/0823-BKS-Meloy/0823-BKS-Meloy-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 297}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': '