ttps://www.nytimes.com/2015/08/22/upshot/a-gay-baseball-player-in-statistical-perspective.html', 'snippet': 'When David Denson came out, it was a first for an active player, but there are probably dozens of gay men playing for teams in the majors and minor leagues.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Until this week, no active baseball player associated with a Major League Baseball team had ever publicly come out as gay. David Denson, a prospect playing for the Helena Brewers in one of baseball’s rookie leagues, has finally ended that streak.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/22/upshot/22UP-Baseball/22UP-Baseball-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 397, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/08/22/upshot/22UP-Baseball/22UP-Baseball-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 397}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/22/upshot/22UP-Baseball/22UP-Baseball-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 678, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/22/upshot/22UP-Baseball/22UP-Baseball-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1356, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/22/upshot/22UP-Baseball/22UP-Baseball-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/08/22/upshot/22UP-Baseball/22UP-Baseball-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/22/upshot/22UP-Baseball/22UP-Baseball-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Gay Baseball Player, in Statistical Perspective', 'kicker': 'Coming Out', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Only One Gay Player? More Likely Dozens', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Baseball', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Homosexuality and Bisexuality', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Denson, David (1995- )', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Social Conditions and Trends', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-08-21T15:08:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Upshot', 'section_name': 'The Upshot', 'byline': {'original': 'By Justin Wolfers', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Justin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wolfers', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/36e5170e-155f-5701-b9cf-2982df356b5b', 'word_count': 540, 'uri': 'nyt://article/36e5170e-155f-5701-b9cf-2982df356b5b'}, {'abstract': 'In three picture books, toys choose to explore their surroundings or view the world from a windowsill.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/08/23/books/review/kevin-henkess-waiting-and-more.html', 'snippet': 'In three picture books, toys choose to explore their surroundings or view the world from a windowsill.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As both subjects and protagonists, animals seem to rule the world of children’s books, and it’s easy to see why. They are cute, recognizable and fun to imitate. They play a part in every child’s life, sometimes a big one. But what about the toys and tools children use every day? These make more occasional literary appearances — in, for instance, classics such as “Corduroy,” about a stuffed bear alone in a department store, or even “The Little Engine That Could,” with its dolls and small clown — but they really deserve to be more frequent stars. Children have immediate associations with their treasured belongings in a way they might not with, say, a capybara. Three new books, all by prominent picture book authors, artfully use worldly possessions to approach larger themes, to varying degrees of success.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '28', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/07/books/review/0823-BKS-Saltzstein1/0823-BKS-Saltzstein1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 287, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/08/07/books/review/0823-BKS-Saltzstein1/0823-BKS-Saltzstein1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 287}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/07/books/review/0823-BKS-Saltzstein1/0823-BKS-Saltzstein1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 490, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/07/books/review/0823-BKS-Saltzstein1/0823-BKS-Saltzstein1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 980, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/07/books/review/0823-BKS-Saltzstein1/0823-BKS-Saltzstein1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/08/07/books/review/0823-BKS-Saltzstein1/0823-BKS-Saltzstein1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 