d', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5f74cd0b-65a9-5ab4-a912-99176ddc6df4', 'word_count': 1083, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5f74cd0b-65a9-5ab4-a912-99176ddc6df4'}, {'abstract': 'Once plastic microbeads enter the water, they attract toxic substances like PCBs and are eaten by fish.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/08/22/opinion/microbeads-the-tiny-orbs-threatening-our-water.html', 'snippet': 'Once plastic microbeads enter the water, they attract toxic substances like PCBs and are eaten by fish.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Plastic is believed to be the main contaminant in the huge garbage gyres that pollute the oceans. Now researchers, led by Sherri Mason of the State University of New York at Fredonia, have found a stunning amount of plastic in the largest freshwater ecosystem on earth, the Great Lakes. And an increasing amount of it consists of the tiny plastic orbs used as abrasives in products like toothpaste and anti-acne lotions.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/22/opinion/22sat3web/22sat3web-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 335, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/08/22/opinion/22sat3web/22sat3web-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 335}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/22/opinion/22sat3web/22sat3web-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 571, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/22/opinion/22sat3web/22sat3web-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1143, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 