: 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/92172cb6-39b3-5445-bcd1-5be2216066cf', 'word_count': 999, 'uri': 'nyt://article/92172cb6-39b3-5445-bcd1-5be2216066cf'}, {'abstract': 'Thailand’s chief of police appeared to be trying to lower public expectations that the main person being sought in the explosion at a shrine would be caught anytime soon.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/08/24/world/asia/bangkok-thailand-shrine-bombing.html', 'snippet': 'Thailand’s chief of police appeared to be trying to lower public expectations that the main person being sought in the explosion at a shrine would be caught anytime soon.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BANGKOK — Thailand’s chief of police said Sunday that those responsible for the deadly attack on a Bangkok shrine last week “may have already fled” the country, apparently trying to lower public expectations that the main person being sought would be caught anytime soon.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'People Sought in Bangkok Bombing May Have Fled Country, Police Say', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Thai Police Say Bombing Culprit May Have Fled Country', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Thailand', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bombs and Explosives', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Fuller, Thomas', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Bangkok (Thailand)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Erawan Shrine (Bangkok, Thailand)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Police', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-08-23T15:54:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Poypiti Amatatham and Thomas Fuller', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Poypiti', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Amatatham', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Thomas', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fuller', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/696396ea-e373-5720-9c18-0e4febc3328e', 'word_count': 517, 'uri': 'nyt://article/696396ea-e373-5720-9c18-0e4febc3328e'}, {'abstract': 'Television highlights.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/08/23/arts/television/whats-on-tv-sunday.html', 'snippet': 'Television highlights.', 'lead_paragraph': '9 P.M. (AMC) FEAR THE WALKING DEAD In this prequel to “The Walking Dead,” a blended family — a tough-love mom (Kim Dickens), a nice-guy stepdad (Cliff Curtis) and their rebellious daughter (Alycia Debnam-Carey) and heroin-addicted son (Frank Dillane) — find themselves outmaneuvering zombies after a flu epidemic in Los Angeles turns out to be so much more, above. But if “The Walking Dead” is one of television’s quietest shows, this one — with the sirens, helicopters, news reports and cellphones of a functioning society — raises the decibels to amp up the suspense. It feels like a crutch, and also “a sign that the show’s creators, who include Robert Kirkman, the impresario of the ‘Walking Dead’ graphic novel and TV franchise, aren’t nearly as sure of their footing this time around,” Mike Hale wrote in The New York Times. And the domestic-drama tropes — teenage rebellion, the messiness of divorce, dealing with addiction — seem tired in this context, he added. “There’s a point being made about the dissolution of everyday society, but watching it being made is like eating your vegetables — you’d rather skip ahead to the flesh.”', 'print_section': 'SP', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What’s On TV Sunday', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'What&#8217;s On TV Sunday', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-08-23T16:05:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kathryn Shattuck', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kathryn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shattuck', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Schedule', '_id': 'nyt://article/53d175d0-c782-5214-980b-71abb02acd40', 'word_count': 623, 'uri': 'nyt://article/53d175d0-c782-5214-980b-71abb02acd40'}, {'abstract': 'For the first time, right near the end, the show feels a little stale.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/08/23/hannibal-recap-season-3-red-dragon-chilton/', 'snippet': 'For the first time, right near the end, the show feels a little stale.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Season 3, Episode 12: “The Number of the Beast Is 666″', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/21/arts/23HANNIBAL/23HANNIBAL-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/08/21/arts/23HANNIBAL/23HANNIBAL-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/21/arts/23HANNIBAL/23HANNIBAL-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/21/arts/23HANNIBAL/23HANNIBAL-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_na