', '_id': 'nyt://article/d75ffaa0-4362-51f8-bf47-cf28c0c3f3fc', 'word_count': 1446, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d75ffaa0-4362-51f8-bf47-cf28c0c3f3fc'}, {'abstract': 'Mr. Burns’s 10-episode series is set in the 1960s in Hell’s Kitchen and somewhere around the corner from Damon Runyon.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/08/25/arts/television/review-edward-burnss-public-morals-on-tnt.html', 'snippet': 'Mr. Burns’s 10-episode series is set in the 1960s in Hell’s Kitchen and somewhere around the corner from Damon Runyon.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Edward Burns’s new series, “Public Morals,” is set in Hell’s Kitchen in the 1960s and filmed in New York, at Silvercup Studios and at locations like the Russian Tea Room, the Park Lane Hotel and Barrow’s Pub in Greenwich Village. It doesn’t seem to take place anywhere in the real world, though. It’s in an imaginary American neighborhood — around the corner from Runyon, down the block from Scorsese, within sight of Mamet — where characters are nicknamed Beantown and Specs and say things like, “I get a one-way ticket to Palookaville.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/25/arts/25MORALS/25MORALS-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 358, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/08/25/arts/25MORALS/25MORALS-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 358}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/25/arts/25MORALS/25MORALS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 611, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/25/arts/25MORALS/25MORALS-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1223, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'lega