, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Black People', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Discrimination', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Race and Ethnicity', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-08-25T11:00:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Claudia Rankine', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Claudia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rankine', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2758e160-fc97-5750-9ad9-d2cc1c674b5c', 'word_count': 2936, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2758e160-fc97-5750-9ad9-d2cc1c674b5c'}, {'abstract': 'This fall, London’s robust tourism industry will commemorate the 75th anniversary of the capital’s time under siege.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/08/30/travel/london-blitz-world-war-2.html', 'snippet': 'This fall, London’s robust tourism industry will commemorate the 75th anniversary of the capital’s time under siege.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Beginning in the autumn of 1940, German Luftwaffe pilots bombed London for 57 consecutive nights. It began on Sept. 7, when 348 German bombers and 617 Messerschmitt fighters appeared in the clear skies above the British capital and began to pound the area with explosives. In the months that followed, great numbers of aghast Londoners fled to the countryside. But many more stayed put, through air raids that flattened great swaths of the city and killed 20,000 residents. When the bomb shelters filled up, tens of thousands of Londoners filed into Underground stations, where they slept, packed like sardines, on the concrete platforms.', 'print_section': 'TR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'For History-Minded Tourists, London Marks Blitz Anniversary', 'kicker': 'Heads Up', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'When the Bombs Rained Down', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'London (England)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'World War II (1939-45)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Great Britain', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-08-25T11:00:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Travel', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By Katie Engelhart', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Katie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Engelhart', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fdb37ec0-3a5d-5b80-bef8-2411d7941230', 'word_count': 789, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fdb37ec0-3a5d-5b80-bef8-2411d7941230'}, {'abstract': 'The city wants to pipe water from the nearby lake to address a problem with its own water. But a 2008 compact prohibits that sort of thing.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/08/26/us/waukesha-plan-for-lake-michigan-water-raises-worries.html', 'snippet': 'The city wants to pipe water from the nearby lake to address a problem with its own water. But a 2008 compact prohibits that sort of thing.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WAUKESHA, Wis. — This city, once famous for its bubbling natural springs, sits about 17 miles from the shore of Lake Michigan. So when the state and federal authorities began demanding that the city address a growing contamination problem in its aquifer, the answer seemed simple: Get water from the big lake.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/26/us/26lakes1/26lakes1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 333, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge