orted', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interactive Feature', '_id': 'nyt://interactive/4e1e81b4-6720-5670-8d8c-22b641eda362', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://interactive/4e1e81b4-6720-5670-8d8c-22b641eda362'}, {'abstract': 'Metropolitan Diary: Moving to the city is not a speed-dating proposition, and a prospective newcomer from far away wants this relationship to be lasting.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/08/25/dear-new-york-will-we-work-out/', 'snippet': 'Metropolitan Diary: Moving to the city is not a speed-dating proposition, and a prospective newcomer from far away wants this relationship to be lasting.', 'lead_paragraph': ' Dear Diary: ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Dear New York, Will We Work Out?', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dating and Relationships', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-08-25T12:30:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Khadija ElDachach', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Khadija', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'ElDachach', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c29d7157-c7ac-5a2d-a2f7-fe9b32d8328d', 'word_count': 218, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c29d7157-c7ac-5a2d-a2f7-fe9b32d8328d'}, {'abstract': 'Times music critics discuss Justin Bieber’s collaboration with Skrillex and Diplo.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/audio/2015/08/25/arts/music/25popcast_pod.html', 'snippet': 'Times music critics discuss Justin Bieber’s collaboration with Skrillex and Diplo.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Times music critics discuss Justin Bieber’s collaboration with Skrillex and Diplo.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Popcast: ‘Where Are Ü Now’', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2015-08-25T12:32:11+0000', 'document_type': 'audio', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://audio/f2b79733-bc09-520b-a4b9-76acc3c1cc25', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://audio/f2b79733-bc09-520b-a4b9-76acc3c1cc25'}, {'abstract': 'Times music critics discuss the intention of the song, and its possible yearning to override predetermined systems of songwriting, technology and artistic persona.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/08/25/arts/music/popcast-where-are-u-now.html', 'snippet': 'Times music critics discuss the intention of the song, and its possible yearning to override predetermined systems of songwriting, technology and artistic persona.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Where Are Ü Now,” released last February and a contender for consensual song-of-the-summer status, was a result of a fairly typical songwriting process: piano chord changes and a sung topline, later developed through digital manipulations into a dance track. The singer was Justin Bieber (who developed the core of the song with his collaborator Poo Bear), and the digital manipulators were Skrillex and Diplo, who were putting together their “Jack U” collaboration album.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Popcast: ‘Where Are Ü Now’', 'kicker': 'Popcast', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bieber, Justin', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Skrillex', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Pentz, Thomas (Diplo)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Boyd, Jason (Poo Bear)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-08-25T12:32:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Ratliff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ratliff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6d367c02-2192-5bf6-a390-befd0afe07c4', 'word_count': 234, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6d367c02-2192-5bf6-a390-befd0afe07c4'}, {'abstract': 'As stocks slumped, sentiment grew among investors that the central bank, which takes a broad view of the economy, would hold off on raising rates this year.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/08/26/business/economy/will-the-fed-raise-interest-rates-anytime-soon-investors-say-no.html', 'snippet': 'As stocks slumped, sentiment grew among investors that the central bank, which takes a broad view of the economy, would hold off on raising rates this year.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — As markets quake and stock prices fall, investors increasingly are betting that the Federal Reserve will not raise interest rates this year.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/25/business/26FED/26FED-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/08/25/business/26FED/26FED-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/25/business/26FED/26FED-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0