m, which features the stand-up comic Jerrod Carmichael, makes an effort to include some controversial topics amid its one-liners.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/08/26/arts/television/review-the-carmichael-show-with-jabs-and-jokes-has-its-debut-on-nbc.html', 'snippet': 'This sitcom, which features the stand-up comic Jerrod Carmichael, makes an effort to include some controversial topics amid its one-liners.', 'lead_paragraph': 'You remember your mother’s admonition: “If you can’t say something nice, don’t say anything at all.” In that spirit, let’s just say that “The Carmichael Show,” a comedy that begins Wednesday on NBC, is marginally better than the awful “Mr. Robinson,” another comedy the network disgorged this month.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/26/arts/26CARMICHAEL-SUB/26CARMICHAEL-SUB-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 391, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/08/26/arts/26CARMICHAEL-SUB/26CARMICHAEL-SUB-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 391}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/26/arts/26CARMICHAEL-SUB/26CARMICHAEL-SUB-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 667, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/26/arts/26CARMICHAEL-SUB/26CARMICHAEL-SUB-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1334, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/26/arts/26CARMICHAEL-SUB/26CARMICHAEL-SUB-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/08/26/arts/26CARMICHAEL-SUB/26CARMICHAEL-SUB-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/26/arts/26CARMICHAEL-SUB/26CARMICHAEL-SUB-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Review: ‘The Carmichael Show,’ With Jabs and Jokes, Has Its Debut on NBC', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Family That Slips In Jabs Amid the Jokes', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Carmichael, Jerrod', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Broadcasting Co', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Comedy and Humor', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Carmichael Show (TV Program)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-08-25T21:42:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Neil Genzlinger', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Neil', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Genzlinger', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/2f14de58-714d-54e3-824f-1d4b4e3866af', 'word_count': 451, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2f14de58-714d-54e3-824f-1d4b4e3866af'}, {'abstract': 'Alex Ross Perry’s narrative experiment, starring Katherine Waterston and Ms. Moss, turns out to be an art house film in quotation marks.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/08/26/movies/review-elisabeth-moss-at-all-angles-in-queen-of-earth.html', 'snippet': 'Alex Ross Perry’s narrative experiment, starring Katherine Waterston and Ms. Moss, turns out to be an art house film in quotation marks.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When you first see Catherine, she’s looking upward, her teary, blotchy face filling the frame. She’s suffering, all right, you better believe it, although she doesn’t seem to be doing a particularly good job of convincing the guy who’s mostly off-camera. She looks like a mess — pathetic, really, with her smeared black eye makeup and the tears that just keep pooling and dribbling. The funny thing is that with her wet raccoon eyes and red nose, Catherine — a sensational Elisabeth Moss — also looks like a sad clown. All that’s missing is the black velvet background. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/26/arts/26QUEEN/26QUEEN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 331, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/08/26/arts/26QUEEN/26QUEEN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 331}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/26/arts/26QUEEN/26QUEEN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 565, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/26/arts/26QUEEN/26QUEEN-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 790, 'width': 1432, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/26/arts/26QUEEN/26QUEEN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/08/26/arts/26QUEEN/26QUEEN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/26/arts/26QUEEN/26QUEEN-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Review: Elisabeth Moss at All Angles in ‘Queen of Earth’', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Two Women in the Country With Extreme Close-Ups', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Perry, Alex Ross', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Moss, Elisabeth', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Waterston, Katherine', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Queen of Earth (Movie)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-08-25T21:43:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Manohla Dargis', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Manohla', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Dargis', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/c60e8ff4-43cf-5f0c-8c75-b0dc6c1c75f7', 'word_count': 865, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c60e8ff4-43cf-5f0c-8c75-b0dc6c1c75f7'}, {'abstract': 'The musician shares the books, bands and bakeries that have helped to shape his work.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/08/25/t-magazine/yo-la-tengo-ira-kaplan-inspirations.html', 'snippet': 'The musician shares the books, bands and bakeries that have helped to shape his work.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Of all the indie bands considered indispensable to the ’80s and ’90s underground, perhaps none have aged more gracefully, or with as little ceremony, as New Jersey’s own Yo La Tengo. The husband-wife team of Ira Kaplan and Georgia Hubley, along with bassist James McNew, who joined the band in 1992, are known equally well for their heart-rending melodies as they are for ear-splitting guitars. For Yo La Tengo’s new record, “Stuff Like That There,” which falls firmly in the former category, the band looks backward to “Fakebook,” an LP that came that out 25 years ago and remains a fan favorite today. Like its predecessor, “Stuff Like That There” is made up predominantly of covers — both of classic acts (Hank Williams, The Cure) and of Yo La Tengo themselves (“Deeper Into Movies,” “The Ballad of Red Buckets”). “We kind of took some of the things from ‘Fakebook’ and said, ‘It’s 25 years later, let’s see what happens if we do it again.’ It’s definitely a companion, in our mind,” Kaplan says.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Yo La Tengo’s Ira Kaplan on 10 of His Influences', 'kicker': 'Entertainment', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Yo La Tengo (Music Group)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-08-25T21:44:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'TStyle', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nathan Reese', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nathan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Reese', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/282fac0f-2ac7-5450-bb16-8e96a4777497', 'word_count': 306, 'uri': 'nyt://article/282fac0f-2ac7-5450-bb16-8e96a4777497'}, {'abstract': 'Downtown views in Maine, a floating house in Seattle and a cabin in Virginia', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/slideshow/2015/08/26/realestate/what-you-get-for-785-000.html', 'snippet': 'Downtown views in Maine, a floating house in Seattle and a cabin in Virginia', 'lead_paragraph': 'Downtown views in Maine, a floating house in Seattle and a cabin in Virginia', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia