 about the joys of going back to Jamaica.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As a celebrated musician and bearer of the most recognizable name in reggae, Damian Marley is often on tour.', 'print_section': 'TR', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/30/travel/30QA/30QA-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 423, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/08/30/travel/30QA/30QA-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 423}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/30/travel/30QA/30QA-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 722, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/30/travel/30QA/30QA-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1444, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/30/travel/30QA/30QA-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/08/30/travel/30QA/30QA-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/30/travel/30QA/30QA-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Jamaica Will Always Be Home for Damian Marley', 'kicker': 'Q&A', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Damian Marley on How Music Breaks Language Barriers.', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Christiansen, Kenan', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Reggae Music', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Kingston (Jamaica)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Marley, Damian', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Jamaica (West Indies)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-08-26T11:00:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Travel', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kenan Christiansen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kenan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Christiansen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Question', '_id': 'nyt://article/73c2c21b-2556-5cfe-8f73-3dfd20565401', 'word_count': 623, 'uri': 'nyt://article/73c2c21b-2556-5cfe-8f73-3dfd20565401'}, {'abstract': 'On the South Side of Chicago, a campaign to diversify the game.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/08/30/magazine/who-gets-to-play-tennis.html', 'snippet': 'On the South Side of Chicago, a campaign to diversify the game.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When the Hyde Park Racquet Club opened in the late 1970s, amid the country’s short-lived tennis boom, there wasn’t another indoor facility in Chicago for 10 miles. The club occupied a section of 47th Street, near the lake, that long served as the boundary between the aging mansions of Kenwood and the greater South Side beyond. Across the street was a record shop that sold house music and a drive-through liquor store that peddled home brew in gallon jugs with the promise ‘‘Won’t go flat!’’ In a hot-dog stand on the block, two teenagers once pummeled me with their fists while three of my tennis buddies could only watch, as we were useless at hitting anything other than yellow balls. The original owners of the club soon bailed. The many racquetball courts they had built went mostly unused. The sauna wasn’t properly ventilated and rotted the locker room. A fitness chain eventually rented the space, filling half the tennis courts with weight benches and treadmills; the other five courts remained playable but were invariably coated in tennis ball fuzz, many of their overhead lights askew and glaring or altogether dark.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '44', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/30/magazine/southside-chicago-tennis-slide-TB6S/southside-chicago-tennis-slide-TB6S-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/08/30/magazine/southside-chicago-tennis-slide-TB6S/southside-chicago-tennis-slide-TB6S-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/30/magazine/southside-chicago-tennis-slide-TB6S/southside-chicago-tennis-slide-TB6S-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/30/magazine/southside-chicago-tennis-slide-TB6S/southside-chicago-tennis-slide-TB6S-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1367, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/30/magazine/southside-chicago-tennis-slide-TB6S/southside-chicago-tennis-slide-TB6S-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/08/30/magazine/southside-chicago-tennis-slide-TB6S/southside-chicago-tennis-slide-TB6S-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thu