 {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Tanzania', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-08-26T14:00:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Africa', 'byline': {'original': 'By Marc Santora', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Marc', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Santora', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a8455177-b0af-5be9-a4ac-1ecf836fc8ef', 'word_count': 1520, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a8455177-b0af-5be9-a4ac-1ecf836fc8ef'}, {'abstract': 'The cookbooks of the past — free of today’s relentless ambition, optimization and ease — remind us what it means to be a human eater in the world.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/08/30/magazine/what-we-learn-from-old-recipes.html', 'snippet': 'The cookbooks of the past — free of today’s relentless ambition, optimization and ease — remind us what it means to be a human eater in the world.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Out with the old and in with the new! Or so the saying goes. But it often goes the opposite way for me: In a reading life full of recipes, I like old ones better. I didn’t realize how much, or why, until I recently started writing a book updating a hundred of them — mostly from the 20th century, but all with timeworn roots.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '32', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/30/magazine/30eat2/30mag-30eat-t_CA3-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 750, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/08/30/magazine/30eat2/30mag-30eat-t_CA3-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 750}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/30/magazine/30eat2/30mag-30eat-t_CA3-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 820, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/30/magazine/30eat2/30mag-30eat-t_CA3-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1639, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/30/magazine/30eat2/30mag-30eat-t_CA3-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/08/30/magazine/30eat2/30mag-30eat-t_CA3-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/30/magazine/30eat2/30mag-30eat-t_CA3-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'What We Learn From Old Recipes', 'kicker': 'Eat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Bygone Ways', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cooking and Cookbooks', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Adler, Tamar', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Shrimp', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Pickles and Relishes', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Recipes', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dumas, Alexandre', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-08-26T14:01:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tamar Adler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tamar', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Adler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d74379c6-e6a8-5bc8-b67c-0c71338cc9cd', 'word_count': 883, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d74379c6-e6a8-5bc8-b67c-0c71338cc9cd'}, {'abstract': 'Mostly true stories form a novel about an 18th-century French expedition across two oceans.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/08/30/books/review/landfalls-by-naomi-j-williams.html', 'snippet': 'Mostly true stories form a novel about an 18th-century French expedition across two oceans.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When the explorers in “Landfalls” confess their purpose — “We were .\u2008.\u2008. ready below with maps outspread and the journals of our predecessors open to the relevant pages, ready on deck and aloft with our keen young eyes and burnished telescopes” — they also describe the parallel joys of history and fiction, those most sympathetic bedfellows. Both demand an engagement with humanity that must skate across time and space to achieve any sort of context, any degree of relevance or reach. The best kind of historical fiction, then, should involve intelligence, experimentation, boldness and curiosity.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/30/books/review/0830-BKS-SMITH-SUB/0830-BKS-SMITH-SUB-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 359, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/08/30/books/review/0830-BKS-SMITH-SUB/0830-BKS-SMITH-SUB-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 359}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/30/books/review/0830-BKS-SMITH-SUB/0830-BKS-SMITH-SUB-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 613, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/30/books/review/0830-BKS-SMITH-SUB/0830-BKS-SMITH-SUB-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1227, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/30/books/review/0830-BKS-SMITH-SUB/0830-BKS-SMITH-SUB-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/08/30/books/review/0830-BKS-SMITH-SUB/0830-BKS-SMITH-SUB-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/30/books/review/0830-BKS-SMITH-SUB/0830-BKS-SMITH-SUB-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘Landfalls,’ by Naomi J. Williams', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Laden Voyage', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Williams, Naomi J (1964- )', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Landfalls (Book)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-08-26T14:12:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Katy Simpson Smith', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Katy', 'middlename': 'Simpson', 'lastname': 'Smith', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/f87efb05-564a-58ce-b798-5ba2a3d4a089', 'word_count': 988, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f87efb05-564a-58ce-b798-5ba2a3d4a089'}, {'abstract': 'Naomi J. Williams couldn’t have predicted the nature of her debut novel: “I’m just the most unlikely person to have written this book.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/08/30/books/review/an-unlikely-debut.html', 'snippet': 'Naomi J. Williams couldn’t have predicted the nature of her debut novel: “I’m just the most unlikely person to have written this book.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'A map changed Naomi J. Williams’s life. The author of the debut novel “Landfalls,” reviewed this week, received an antique map of Alaska from her husband, and became curious about two ships listed on it: the Boussole and the Astrolabe. “Next thing I knew,” she said in an email interview, “a decade had passed and I had this book.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/30/books/review/30OPENBOOK/30OPENBOOK-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/08/30/books/review/30OPENBOOK/30OPENBOOK-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 450}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/30/books/review/30OPENBOOK/30OPENBOOK-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 767, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/30/books/review/30OPENBOOK/30OPENBOOK-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1508, 'width': 2012, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/30/books/review/30OPENBOOK/30OPENBOOK-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/08/30/books/review/30OPENBOOK/30OPENBOOK-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge