r, Matthew', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tennis', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Nadal, Rafael', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hilfiger, Tommy', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fashion and Apparel', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-08-26T16:44:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Styles', 'section_name': 'Fashion & Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Matthew Schneier', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Matthew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schneier', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5a470b15-d77d-5351-b389-1c3787c95120', 'word_count': 547, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5a470b15-d77d-5351-b389-1c3787c95120'}, {'abstract': 'The singer-songwriter is playing Rockwood Music Hall in advance of the Sept. 25 release of her second album.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/08/30/arts/music/amy-bezunarteas-new-villain-taps-a-discomfort-zone-of-terse-insights.html', 'snippet': 'The singer-songwriter is playing Rockwood Music Hall in advance of the Sept. 25 release of her second album.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A clear voice and an unsparing eye define the songs of Amy Bezunartea, who is playing Rockwood Music Hall in advance of the Sept. 25 release of her calmly implacable second album, “New Villain” (Kiam). She keeps the music slow and sparse, sometimes just a lone guitar or piano note, deliberately exposing the way her terse lyrics and finely chiseled melodies carry uncomfortable insights about herself and others.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/30/arts/30GUIDESUB3A/30GUIDESUB3-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 600, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/08/30/arts/30GUIDESUB3A/30GUIDESUB3-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 600}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/30/arts/30GUIDESUB3A/30GUIDESUB3-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 640, 'width': 640, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/30/arts/30GUIDESUB3A/30GUIDESUB3-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 640, 'width': 640, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/30/arts/30GUIDESUB3A/30GUIDESUB3-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/08/30/arts/30GUIDESUB3A/30GUIDESUB3-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/30/arts/30GUIDESUB3A/30GUIDESUB3-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Amy Bezunartea’s ‘New Villain’ Taps a Discomfort Zone of Terse Insights', 'kicker': 'Pop', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Discomfort Zone of Terse Insights', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Rockwood Music Hall', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bezunartea, Amy (1978- )', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-08-26T16:45:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jon Pareles', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pareles', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/29a5b77b-a225-5798-adee-bdd74c72e490', 'word_count': 146, 'uri': 'nyt://article/29a5b77b-a225-5798-adee-bdd74c72e490'}, {'abstract': 'The documentary programs “Warlords of Ivory” and “Elephant Queen” explore human threats and environmental ones.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/08/30/arts/television/national-geographics-tv-outlets-examine-elephants-plight.html', 'snippet': 'The documentary programs “Warlords of Ivory” and “Elephant Queen” explore human threats and environmental ones.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It has been a summer full of shark programming, but National Geographic’s outlets are devoting the last Sunday of August to elephants and their struggles to survive. At 8 p.m., the National Geographic Channel brings back “Explorer” with an episode called “Warlords of Ivory,” a disturbing look at how extreme elephant poaching is in Africa. The journalist Bryan Christy puts a tracking device inside a fake tusk in an effort to determine what path it travels on its way to the black market, and who profits as it does. Mr. Christy has a self-aggrandizing style, but the episode demonstrates that the ivory trade is no longer limited to a lone poacher with a rifle; mass slaughter with automatic weapons shot from helicopters is part of the problem.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/30/arts/30GUIDESUB8/30GUIDESUB8-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/08/30/arts/30GUIDESUB8/30GUIDESUB8-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/30/arts/30GUIDESUB8/30GUIDESUB8-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/30/arts/30GUIDESUB8/30GUIDESUB8-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/30/arts/30GUIDESUB8/30GUIDESUB8-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/08/30/arts/30GUIDESUB8/30GUIDESUB8-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/30/arts/30GUIDESUB8/30GUIDESUB8-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'National Geographic’s TV Outlets Examine Elephant’s Plight', 'kicker': 'Television', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Hounded by Guns and Drought', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elephants', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Geographic Channel', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-08-26T16:48:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Neil Genzlinger', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Neil', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Genzlinger', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e24cdcef-d3a0-5090-8352-cd8bf690be85', 'word_count': 194, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e24cdcef-d3a0-5090-8352-cd8bf690be85'}, {'abstract': 'Whether back-to-school means college or high school, apps can help a student stay organized and productive.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/video/technology/personaltech/100000003874975/app-smart-start-school-right.html', 'snippet': 'Whether back-to-school means college or high school, apps can help a student stay organized and productive.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Whether back-to-school means college or high school, apps can help a student stay organized and productive.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/26/multimedia/appsmart-back-to-school/appsmart-back-to-school-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 338, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/08/26/multimedia/appsmart-back-to-school/appsmart-back-to-school-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 338}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/26/multimedia/appsmart-back-to-school/appsmart-back-to-school-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 576, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/26/multimedia/appsmart-back-to-school/appsmart-back-to-school-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1080, 'width': 1920, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/26/multimedia/appsmart-back-to-school/appsmart-back-to-school-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/08/26/multimedia/appsmart-back-to-school/appsmart-back-to-school-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/26/multimedia/appsmart-back-to-school/appsmart-back-to-school-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'App Smart | Start School Right', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mobile Applications', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Education', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Education (K-12)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ADULT EDUCATION', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Colleges and Universities', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-08-26T16:48:37+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'subsection_name': 'Personal Tech', 'byline': {'original': 'By KIT EATON and DALLAS JENSEN', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kit', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'EATON', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Dallas', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'JENSEN', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/3fad04a0-f302-599e-9919-ba78d7e440a3', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/3fad04a0-f302-599e-9919-ba78d7e440a3'}, {'abstract': 'Make your own flashcards and other study aids, and keep on schedule with some education apps.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/08/27/technology/personaltech/video-feature-some-back-to-school-tools-to-download.html', 'snippet': 'Make your own flashcards and other study aids, and keep on schedule with some education apps.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Students enjoying their last days of summer break have the threat of back-to-school time hanging over their heads, like the sword of Damocles. If that Damocles reference has you confused, why not look up the ancient story using the Google app? Then continue reading this column to discover other apps that can help students with their studies and make the back-to-school transition smoother.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Video Feature: Back-to-School Apps for the Organized Student', 'kicker': 'App Smart', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Back-to-School Tools for the Organized Student', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mobile Applications', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Eaton, Kit', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Education (K-12)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Android (Operating System)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'iOS (Operating System)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Education', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-08-26T16:48:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'subsection_name': 'Personal Tech', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kit Eaton', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kit', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Eaton', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7efbff2c-eb0c-5c3b-a250-673ba2d14a2e', 'word_count': 725, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7efbff2c-eb0c-5c3b-a250-673ba2d14a2e'}, {'abstract': '“Transmissions: Art in Eastern Europe and Latin America, 1960-1980” will present some 300 artworks in numerous mediums, many on view for the first time.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/08/30/arts/design/moma-presents-an-exhibition-on-eastern-european-and-latin-american-art.html', 'snippet': '“Transmissions: Art in Eastern Europe and Latin America, 1960-1980” will present some 300 artworks in numerous mediums, many on view for the first time.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It’s always interesting to learn what a rich, acquisitive institution like the Museum of Modern Art has added to its collection. The latest report is the exhibition “Transmissions: Art in Eastern Europe and Latin America, 1960-1980,” which opens on September 5 and will present some 300 artworks in numerous mediums, many on view for the first time. Nearly all date from around 1968, a period when art radically diversified, sometimes dematerializing altogether. These works represent artists from distant regions whose efforts ran parallel in terms of strategies (largely Conceptual, frequently performance-oriented), political contexts (often oppressive) and interests (protest, mass media, popular art). Some names — Valie Export, Ana Mendieta, Edward Krasinski, Juan Downey, Fernando Botero — will be familiar; others will be much less so. The show will surely reflect the MoMA’s desire for a broad global reach, but perhaps also the aesthetic narrowness of its relentless elevation of Conceptual-oriented work. (Through Jan. 3, moma.org.)', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/30/arts/30GUIDE5/30GUIDE5-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 725, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/08/30/arts/30GUIDE5/30GUIDE5-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 725}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/30/arts/30GUIDE5/30GUIDE5-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 848, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype':