pet': 'The announcement signals a reversal of Washington’s longstanding insistence on an international inquiry into possible war crimes during the final stages of the island’s civil war.', 'lead_paragraph': 'COLOMBO, Sri Lanka — The United States will sponsor a resolution on Sri Lanka next month at the United Nations Human Rights Council backing Colombo’s plans for reconciliation and to conduct an investigation into possible war crimes during the final stages of the island’s civil war, a top American diplomat said Wednesday.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'U.S. to Support Sri Lanka Plan to Investigate War Crimes', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'U.S. to Support Sri Lanka Plan to Investigate War Crimes', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Sri Lanka', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'War Crimes, Genocide and Crimes Against Humanity', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Colombo (Sri Lanka)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States International Relations', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'United Nations Human Rights Council', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Liberation Tigers of Tamil Eelam', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rajapaksa, Mahinda', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sirisena, Maithripala', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wickremesinghe, Ranil', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-08-26T19:44:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dharisha Bastians', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dharisha', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bastians', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a8ab7ed7-bad7-5764-9f61-7335b107efec', 'word_count': 407, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a8ab7ed7-bad7-5764-9f61-7335b107efec'}, {'abstract': 'Poems take on the political, from oil spills to Occupy Oakland. ', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/08/30/books/review/that-winter-the-wolf-came-by-juliana-spahr.html', 'snippet': 'Poems take on the political, from oil spills to Occupy Oakland. ', 'lead_paragraph': 'Nobody has to tell Juliana Spahr that the personal is political: She has spent her career inventing verse and prose forms that show how our intimate feelings emerge, like knots in nets or waves at sea, from systems much larger than any one person’s control. “This Connection of Everyone With Lungs” (2005) \xadexamined the global effects of 9/11 through the metaphor of polluted air; the piled-up details in “Well Then There Now” (2011) looked at Spahr’s years in Hawaii, and at her \xadorigins in Appalachia, as if to ask where she could call home.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/30/books/review/30POWERS/30POWERS-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 890, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/08/30/books/review/30POWERS/30POWERS-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 890}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/30/books/review/30POWERS/30POWERS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 691, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/30/books/review/30POWERS/30POWERS-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1381, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/30/books/review/30POWERS/30POWERS-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/08/30/books/review/30POWERS/30POWERS-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/30/books/review/30POWERS/30POWERS-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘That Winter the Wolf Came,’ by Juliana Spahr', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Drilling Deep', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Burt, Stephen', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Poetry and Poets', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Spahr, Juliana (1966- )', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'That Winter the Wolf Came (Book)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-08-26T19:44:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stephen Burt', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stephen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Burt', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/4a065d58-c0f6-56ba-b44b-354f5532ac43', 'word_count': 630, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4a065d58-c0f6-56ba-b44b-354f5532ac43'}, {'abstract': 'A writer with a cult following has turned her life into six well-loved novels. But where is the last one?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/08/30/books/review/harriet-wolfs-seventh-book-of-wonders-by-julianna-baggott.html', 'snippet': 'A writer with a cult following has turned her life into six well-loved novels. But where is the last one?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Many things are hidden in Julianna Baggott’s intricate, tenderhearted novel about a writer, her children and a legacy of loss. Love letters, folded into origami cranes that never take flight, are tucked under a mattress. A newborn child, mute and sallow, is spirited away from her mother and secreted in an institution. A young man supports himself as a professional hider of things, until he too must go into hiding. A wife is lost, a husband is lost, a mother is lost and so is a father. Indeed, an entire book, Harriet Wolf’s seventh novel, has disappeared. And, like so many precious lost things, it may be hiding in plain sight. All this sounds somber. But in groping for what’s lost, Baggott’s characters also stumble across pleasure, joy — and recognition.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '21', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/30/books/review/30BROWNING/30BROWNING-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 900, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/08/30/books/review/30BROWNING/30BROWNING-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 900}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/30/books/review/30BROWNING/30BROWNING-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 683, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/30/books/review/30BROWNING/30BROWNING-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1366, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 