es of Personal Technology', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wireless Communications', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Gogo LLC', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-08-27T18:30:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'subsection_name': 'Personal Tech', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brian X. Chen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brian', 'middlename': 'X.', 'lastname': 'Chen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/15f5969b-744e-5c97-9f0b-cbd92186cd4f', 'word_count': 277, 'uri': 'nyt://article/15f5969b-744e-5c97-9f0b-cbd92186cd4f'}, {'abstract': 'Kari Soinio manipulates his photos to create a Manhattan that seems, as he says, “both familiar and utterly foreign.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/08/27/t-magazine/kari-soinio-new-york-photographs.html', 'snippet': 'Kari Soinio manipulates his photos to create a Manhattan that seems, as he says, “both familiar and utterly foreign.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'When he landed in New York City as a first-time tourist in 1990, the Finnish photographer Kari Soinio — like so many visitors before and after him — was struck by the breathtaking sweep of the Manhattan skyline. When he returned to live downtown for an artist residency in 2006, his gaze was again drawn upward. “I was amazed by the canyon of high-rises,” he says, “and the whole idea of the newcomer looking up.” It was with this view in mind that he embarked on an ongoing series, “City of Ghosts,” which combines street and architectural photography. Soinio captures the beauty of the city’s renowned landmarks — the Washington Square Arch, the Flatiron and Woolworth buildings — and pulls them into the foreground, while unwitting passersby become shadowy, anonymous characters in a duet between pedestrian and monument. Shooting with a 4x5-inch camera with no viewfinder, the defocused scene is “both familiar and utterly foreign,” as Soinio puts it, thanks to his manipulation of the images’ depths of field. “In the happy accident of photography, if you really know your camera, you can do so much,” he says. As “City of Ghosts” has continued, Soinio has shifted his attention to the changing urban landscape and the new glass towers that now reach skyward. In an age during which it’s never been more tempting to keep your eyes glued to a screen while traversing the urban landscape, his photos are a welcome reminder: Look up!', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Haunting Images That Make New York Landmarks Feel New Again', 'kicker': 'Viewfinder', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Soinio, Kari (1962- )', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-08-27T18:31:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'TStyle', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Betsy Horan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Betsy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Horan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0309c15b-38c1-57e1-a037-bf5aeed428df', 'word_count': 244, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0309c15b-38c1-57e1-a037-bf5aeed428df'}, {'abstract': 'An analysis of dust from 1,200 homes across the continental United States provides new information about the fungal and bacterial communities within.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/09/01/science/a-homes-story-told-in-dust-bacteria-and-fungi.html', 'snippet': 'An analysis of dust from 1,200 homes across the continental United States provides new information about the fungal and bacterial communities within.', 'lead_paragraph': 'An analysis of dust from 1,200 homes across the continental United States provides new information about the fungal and bacterial communities sharing the great indoors with us.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/28/science/01obs-dust/01obs-dust-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/08/28/science/01obs-dust/01obs-dust-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 450}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/28/science/01obs-dust/01obs-dust-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 768, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/28/science/01obs-dust/01obs-dust-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1535, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/28/science/01obs-dust/01obs-dust-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/08/28/science/01obs-dust/01obs-dust-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/28/science/01obs-dust/01obs-dust-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Home’s Story, Told in Dust, Bacteria and Fungi', 'kicker': 'Observator