 'lead_paragraph': 'A male túngara frog calling within a chorus in Gamboa, Panama, in search of a potential mate.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/26/multimedia/tungara-frog/tungara-frog-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 449, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/08/26/multimedia/tungara-frog/tungara-frog-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 449}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/26/multimedia/tungara-frog/tungara-frog-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 766, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/26/multimedia/tungara-frog/tungara-frog-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1122, 'width': 1500, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/26/multimedia/tungara-frog/tungara-frog-thumbStandard.gif', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/08/26/multimedia/tungara-frog/tungara-frog-thumbStandard.gif', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/26/multimedia/tungara-frog/tungara-frog-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Male Túngara Frog’s Mating Signal', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Reproduction (Biological)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Panama', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-08-27T19:43:37+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Science', 'byline': {'original': 'By AMANDA M. LEA', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Amanda', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/a4f83fb3-fe99-5be4-a1cf-6ea2100f9d17', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/a4f83fb3-fe99-5be4-a1cf-6ea2100f9d17'}, {'abstract': 'Given a choice of two males, a female will pick the most attractive. But, researchers say, that’s not necessarily the case when a third male is added.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/09/01/science/a-twist-to-how-a-tungara-frog-finds-her-prince.html', 'snippet': 'Given a choice of two males, a female will pick the most attractive. But, researchers say, that’s not necessarily the case when a third male is added.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Given a choice of two males, female túngara frogs will select the most attractive — those with calls of a low frequency and long duration. But when a third male who is inferior is added to the lineup, researchers have found, females often choose the less attractive of the first two. In a study published in the journal Science, scientists at the University of Texas, Austin, and at the Smithsonian Tropical Research Institute suggest that when a social situation becomes too complex, obvious decisions become too burdensome for the frogs. A similar effect has been observed in humans, suggesting that irrationality in mate selection may have deep biological roots.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Twist to How a Túngara Frog Finds Her Prince', 'kicker': 'Observatory', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Amphibians: A Twist to How a Túngara Frog Finds Her Prince', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Frogs', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Reproduction (Biological)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Science (Journal)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-08-27T19:43:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Science', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sindya N. Bhanoo', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sindya', 'middlename': 'N.', 'lastname': 'Bhanoo', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/46c3fbac-147e-5f48-bdaf-be808955c3bb', 'word_count': 109, 'uri': 'nyt://article/46c3fbac-147e-5f48-bdaf-be808955c3bb'}, {'abstract': 'In Anna Muylaert’s Brazilian drama, the maid’s teenage daughter comes to visit and doesn’t seem to care for a lot of the rules.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/08/28/movies/review-in-the-second-mother-a-guest-flouts-boundaries.html', 'snippet': 'In Anna Muylaert’s Brazilian drama, the maid’s teenage daughter comes to visit and doesn’t seem to care for a lot of the rules.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THE SECOND MOTHER', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/28/arts/28SECOND/28SECOND-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 431, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/08/28/arts/28SECOND/28SECOND-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 431}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/28/arts/28SECOND/28SECOND-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 736, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/201