15-08-27T21:37:37+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By THE ASSOCIATED PRESS', 'person': [], 'organization': 'THE ASSOCIATED PRESS'}, 'type_of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/edd63265-fbfc-5116-9891-967cc857266e', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/edd63265-fbfc-5116-9891-967cc857266e'}, {'abstract': 'The week leading up to the tennis tournament is a marketing extravaganza for companies represented by elite players.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/08/28/business/media/us-open-stars-juggle-preparation-with-publicity.html', 'snippet': 'The week leading up to the tennis tournament is a marketing extravaganza for companies represented by elite players.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Nike organized street tennis for Maria Sharapova, Roger Federer and others in the meatpacking district. Tommy Hilfiger opted for strip tennis with Rafael Nadal in Bryant Park. Delta Air Lines held a celebrity karaoke night with Serena Williams near Times Square. And American Express staged what it called a “hydro-interactive tennis experience” featuring Ms. Sharapova along the Hudson River.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'U.S. Open Stars Juggle Preparation With Publicity', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Advantage, Brands', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tennis', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rothenberg, Ben', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ADVERTISING AND MARKETING', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Open (Tennis)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Public Relations and Publicity', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-08-27T21:38:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sydney Ember and Ben Rothenberg', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sydney', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ember', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rothenberg', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d009667b-972c-5661-b47f-a1a0f57b1acb', 'word_count': 1091, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d009667b-972c-5661-b47f-a1a0f57b1acb'}, {'abstract': 'This museum show in Boston illuminates the influence of Asia on the arts of colonial North, Central and South America.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/08/28/arts/design/review-made-in-the-americas-explores-globalism-from-way-way-back.html', 'snippet': 'This museum show in Boston illuminates the influence of Asia on the arts of colonial North, Central and South America.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BOSTON — One of the great gifts that multiculturalist thinking gave us was final freedom from the tyranny of purity. Simply put, that old ideal was revealed as an illusion. It doesn’t exist, at least not in art. There, in reality, everything is a mix. Wonderfully, the longer and closer we look, the richer the mix becomes. And this has always been true. Globalism, which we take to be so 21st century, with its networks and mash-ups, is as old as the hills. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/28/arts/28AMERICAS1/28AMERICAS1-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 508, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/08/28/arts/28AMERICAS1/28AMERICAS1-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 508}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit':