it-against-homeland-security.html', 'snippet': 'The department reached a settlement with the top lawyer in the Immigration and Customs Enforcement agency in Arizona, who had claimed discrimination.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Department of Homeland Security has reached a settlement with the top lawyer in the Immigration and Customs Enforcement agency in Arizona, who sued the department in 2014 claiming discrimination. The lawyer, Patricia M. Vroom, said she was bullied and harassed by officials in Washington because she raised concerns about an Obama administration policy applying prosecutorial discretion to spare some immigrants from deportation. Ms. Vroom will be paid $399,999 to end the suit, and a recent annual performance rating will be revised upward to “achieved excellence.” She had said top legal officials lowered her rating after she moved cautiously to allow the release of some undocumented immigrants with criminal records, a rebuke she said amounted to age and sex discrimination. Her lawyer said that as part of the agreement, Ms. Vroom would retire on Oct. 1 after 30 years working for the federal government. Homeland Security officials did not admit wrongdoing. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Arizona: Lawyer Paid to End Suit Against Homeland Security', 'kicker': 'National Briefing | Southwest', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Arizona: Lawyer Is Paid to End Suit Against Homeland Security', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Preston, Julia', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Immigration and Customs Enforcement (US)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Homeland Security Department', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Vroom, Patricia M', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Discrimination', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'ARIZONA', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-08-29T01:10:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Julia Preston', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Julia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Preston', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/2b4a7139-ba16-507b-9130-8e043647e0fc', 'word_count': 151, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2b4a7139-ba16-507b-9130-8e043647e0fc'}, {'abstract': 'Readers recall the experience of relatives.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/08/29/opinion/dementia-and-the-breakdown-of-moral-judgment.html', 'snippet': 'Readers recall the experience of relatives.', 'lead_paragraph': 'To the Editor:', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Dementia and the Breakdown of Moral Judgment', 'kicker': 'Letters', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Dementia and the Breakdown of Moral Judgment', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dementia', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-08-29T01:10:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Letters', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/ca7f400d-05aa-5369-a44c-b884e72f0064', 'word_count': 310, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ca7f400d-05aa-5369-a44c-b884e72f0064'}, {'abstract': 'Corporate parents may have to take responsibility for franchise workers, giving a big lift to the Fight for $15 movement.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/08/29/opinion/a-ruling-with-a-bonus-for-fast-food-workers.html', 'snippet': 'Corporate parents may have to take responsibility for franchise workers, giving a big lift to the Fight for $15 movement.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The “Fight for $15” campaign is about more than higher pay for fast-food workers. It is also about fighting for the right of workers to form strong unions without retaliation. That goal looks much more achievable this week after a National Labor Relations Board ruling that may force corporate parents, like McDonald’s or Yum Brands, to take responsibility for workers at franchises.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/29/opinion/29sat3web/29sat3web-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 415, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/08/29/opinion/29sat3web/29sat3web-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 415}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/29/opinion/29sat3web/29sat3web-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 709, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/29/opinion/29sat3web/29sat3web-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1418, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/29/opinion/29sat3web/29sat3web-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/08/29/opinion/29sat3web/29sat3web-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/29/opinion/29sat3web/29sat3web-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Ruling With a Bonus for 