rganization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/2b4335f2-73a7-599e-a6df-f4e5d70f187a', 'word_count': 604, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2b4335f2-73a7-599e-a6df-f4e5d70f187a'}, {'abstract': 'Researchers say, cut calories. But they don’t take into account what that means over the long term.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/08/30/opinion/diet-advice-that-ignores-hunger.html', 'snippet': 'Researchers say, cut calories. But they don’t take into account what that means over the long term.', 'lead_paragraph': 'TOWARD the end of the Second World War, researchers at the University of Minnesota began a legendary experiment on the psychology and physiology of human starvation — and, thus, on hunger. The subjects were 36 conscientious objectors, some lean, some not. For 24 weeks, these men were semi-starved, fed not quite 1,600 calories a day of foods chosen to represent the fare of European famine areas: “whole-wheat bread, potatoes, cereals and considerable amounts of turnips and cabbage” with “token amounts” of meat and dairy.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/30/opinion/sunday/30taubes/30taubes-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 362, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/08/30/opinion/sunday/30taubes/30taubes-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 362}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/30/opinion/sunday/30taubes/30taubes-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 618, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/30/opinion/sunday/30taubes/30taubes-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1236, 'width': 2048, 'subTyp