ederal Communications Commission', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television Sets and Media Devices', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cable Television', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Consumer Behavior', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Regulation and Deregulation of Industry', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-08-31T07:20:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/d6ca004f-1743-5f1d-a0ca-311e6a44a53a', 'word_count': 591, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d6ca004f-1743-5f1d-a0ca-311e6a44a53a'}, {'abstract': 'If hospitals and the medical community won’t police incompetent physicians, who will?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/08/31/opinion/when-bad-doctors-happen-to-good-patients.html', 'snippet': 'If hospitals and the medical community won’t police incompetent physicians, who will?', 'lead_paragraph': 'ONLY in Albany can a bill pass the Assembly with overwhelming bipartisan support, be sponsored by a majority of the State Senate, be endorsed by Gov. Andrew M. Cuomo, and yet never come up for a final vote. That happened to Lavern’s Law, a bill that would have helped grievously injured victims of medical malpractice have their day in court. This summer, the Senate majority leader, John J. Flanagan, a Republican, wouldn’t allow the bill to be voted on, effectively killing it.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'When Bad Doctors Happen to Good Patients', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Contributors', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Legislative Malpractice', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Moore, Thomas', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name':