conomic zombie in action. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The China Debt Zombie', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2015-08-31T13:02:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b0a2e7ca-18ed-5102-a61c-0486f89d5c9f', 'word_count': 163, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b0a2e7ca-18ed-5102-a61c-0486f89d5c9f'}, {'abstract': 'In the past year, 182 bills intended to protect student data have been introduced in 46 states as legislators attempt to draw a red line around the privacy of children.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/08/31/daily-report-educational-software-gets-attention-from-state-politicians/', 'snippet': 'In the past year, 182 bills intended to protect student data have been introduced in 46 states as legislators attempt to draw a red line around the privacy of children.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Children throughout the United States are heading back to school and many of them — some for the first time — will be using a range of online learning tools. Unfortunately, it is not always clear what the companies providing those tools are allowed to do with the information they glean from students.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/11/30/technology/bits-daily-report/bits-daily-report-articleLarge-v4.png', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/11/30/technology/bits-daily-report/bits-daily-report-articleLarge-v4.png',