fundamental gentleness, even in intense passages, but he brings a precision to the relentless pricks and bristling repetitions in these pieces.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Summer school has been in session in New York piano recitals. Recently, at the Kaplan Penthouse at Lincoln Center, Pierre-Laurent Aimard gave a lesson in canons and fugues, a prelude to his performance of George Benjamin’s combination of those two forms in “Shadowlines.” And on Aug. 24 at another intimate space, Le Poisson Rouge, Taka Kigawa held a class on reverberation and resonance.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/09/01/arts/01KIGAWA/01KIGAWA-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 352, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/09/01/arts/01KIGAWA/01KIGAWA-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 352}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/09/01/arts/01KIGAWA/01KIGAWA-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 600, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/09/01/arts/01KIGAWA/01KIGAWA-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1200, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/09/01/arts/01KIGAWA/01KIGAWA-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/09/01/arts/01KIGAWA/01KIGAWA-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/09/01/arts/01KIGAWA/01KIGAWA-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Review: From Taka Kigawa, a Class on Resonance at Le Poisson Rouge', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Lingering Sonorities, Long After the Final Notes ', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Woolfe, Zachary', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Le Poisson Rouge', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kigawa, Taka', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-08-31T22:10:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Zachary Woolfe', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Zachary', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Woolfe', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/cb14166c-9a20-55ac-8a7f-7494e869541d', 'word_count': 477, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cb14166c-9a20-55ac-8a7f-7494e869541d'}, {'abstract': 'The 20th installment of Mr. Child’s Reacher series presents a gutsy new challenge for its hero, and offers a faint indication that something about him may change.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/09/01/books/review-in-make-me-lee-child-adds-another-layer-to-jack-reacher.html', 'snippet': 'The 20th installment of Mr. Child’s Reacher series presents a gutsy new challenge for its hero, and offers a faint indication that something about him may change.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Grain, meet the railroad” Jack Reacher tells himself, after he’s picked the latest sinister little Nowheresville in which to spend a book’s length of time. He appears to have gotten off a train in the middle of wheat country, for no better reasons than he liked the cryptic name of the town, Mother’s Rest, and that he’s got foolproof instincts for sniffing out trouble. Lee Child’s Reacher series has hit Book No. 20 with a resounding peal of wisecracking glee (“Are you going to be a problem?” “I’m already a problem. The question is, what are you going to do about it?”). Everything about it, starting with Reacher’s nose for bad news, is as strong as ever.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/09/01/arts/01BOOKCHILD/01BOOKCHILD-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 772, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/09/01/arts/01BOOKCHILD/01BOOKCHILD-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 772}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/09/01/arts/01BOOKCHILD/01BOOKCHILD-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 797, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/09/01/arts/01BOOKCHILD/01BOOKCHILD-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1593, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/09/01/arts/01BOOKCHILD/01BOOKCHILD-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/09/01/arts/01BOOKCHILD/01BOOKCHILD-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/09/01/arts/01BOOKCHILD/01BOOKCHILD-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Review: In ‘Make Me,’ Lee Child Adds 